00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:27,560
The first thing I would like to tell you,

9
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:30,640
which I did not mention the last time,

10
00:00:30,640 --> 00:00:34,560
that in the reading assignment, the syllabus that we handed out,

11
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:36,840
and by the way, does everybody have one?

12
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:39,960
If you don't, we have some extras here.

13
00:00:39,960 --> 00:00:40,880
You all have one.

14
00:00:40,880 --> 00:00:41,720
Good.

15
00:00:41,720 --> 00:00:47,360
Anyway, as you can see on the first page,

16
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:52,600
there is a section called preparatory reading.

17
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:57,520
So I would recommend that if you can,

18
00:00:57,520 --> 00:01:01,040
to each time read this recommended reading, which

19
00:01:01,040 --> 00:01:04,920
is available, of course, on the internet,

20
00:01:04,920 --> 00:01:10,160
and that will make it easier for you to follow the lecture.

21
00:01:10,160 --> 00:01:14,800
Because this course, as both in vision and in audition,

22
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:18,400
relies on imparting a great many facts

23
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:21,320
about the work use of these two systems.

24
00:01:21,320 --> 00:01:26,640
And it's easier to be able to memorize these facts

25
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:30,080
by doing the preparatory reading if you can.

26
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:32,800
All right, so today, then, what we are going to do

27
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:34,000
is we're going to initially start

28
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,080
to talk about the layout of the visual system.

29
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:40,560
And then we're going to talk about the retina

30
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:42,360
for much of the course.

31
00:01:42,360 --> 00:01:44,240
At the very end, we'll talk a little bit

32
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:47,520
about the lateral geniculate nucleus.

33
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:49,880
In each case, I will try to tell you

34
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:52,680
about how some people have thought about things

35
00:01:52,680 --> 00:01:57,640
and what the progression is of the discoveries that

36
00:01:57,640 --> 00:01:59,880
had been made about the visual system.

37
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:02,420
It is certainly an area of research

38
00:02:02,420 --> 00:02:05,320
which has been incredibly successful

39
00:02:05,320 --> 00:02:09,800
and has resulted in quite a number of Nobel prizes

40
00:02:09,800 --> 00:02:12,080
for the discoveries that individuals

41
00:02:12,080 --> 00:02:16,160
had made in uncovering the workings of this system.

42
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:19,120
Furthermore, many of the uncoverings

43
00:02:19,120 --> 00:02:26,360
that investigators had done came about as incredible surprises

44
00:02:26,360 --> 00:02:30,520
because so many of the findings were quite unexpected.

45
00:02:30,520 --> 00:02:38,080
And I will try to point these things out during the course.

46
00:02:38,080 --> 00:02:40,440
And my section, as I've mentioned before,

47
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:42,880
will consist of 12 lectures.

48
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:47,800
And then that will be followed on October 23

49
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:49,600
by a midterm exam.

50
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:52,040
And again, to reiterate, that's going

51
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:56,240
to consist of a series of multiple-choice questions.

52
00:02:56,240 --> 00:02:59,800
And then following that, we'll move on

53
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:07,360
to talk about the auditory system that Chris Brown is going

54
00:03:07,360 --> 00:03:09,200
to be presenting to you.

55
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:11,960
All right, so the first thing I want

56
00:03:11,960 --> 00:03:15,360
to do then is to talk about the basic wiring

57
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:18,720
of the visual system, which in itself

58
00:03:18,720 --> 00:03:22,840
has yielded a number of unexpected surprises

59
00:03:22,840 --> 00:03:25,440
and also raised some interesting questions.

60
00:03:25,440 --> 00:03:27,960
So what I want to start with, first of all,

61
00:03:27,960 --> 00:03:31,680
is that when you look at some animals that

62
00:03:31,680 --> 00:03:37,200
have sideways-looking eyes, like the rabbit and most amphibia,

63
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:40,160
the two eyes look out in two different directions,

64
00:03:40,160 --> 00:03:41,760
to the left and to the right.

65
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:45,520
And that enables them to see a very large portion

66
00:03:45,520 --> 00:03:48,080
of the visual field.

67
00:03:48,080 --> 00:03:52,720
And when that is the case, as in this picture shown here

68
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:55,720
for the rabbit, what you find is that there's only

69
00:03:55,720 --> 00:04:01,880
a small area which is seen by both eyes.

70
00:04:01,880 --> 00:04:06,960
But then when we look at higher mammals,

71
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:09,120
and particularly primates and humans,

72
00:04:09,120 --> 00:04:12,040
what you find that there was a huge change

73
00:04:12,040 --> 00:04:16,480
in the course of evolution bringing the eyes to the front.

74
00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:21,440
Now, that change is very interesting.

75
00:04:21,440 --> 00:04:24,680
And we can ask, why on earth did that happen?

76
00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:28,920
Because it meant that, obviously, a human cannot see

77
00:04:28,920 --> 00:04:33,560
behind themselves where these rabbits can see a much larger

78
00:04:33,560 --> 00:04:35,240
portion of the visual field.

79
00:04:35,240 --> 00:04:38,920
And so you sacrifice the ability to see all the way

80
00:04:38,920 --> 00:04:44,240
around the world for being able to see with both eyes

81
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:45,400
to the front.

82
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:48,160
And so the question is, why did this happen?

83
00:04:48,160 --> 00:04:51,360
Now, we are going to discuss this in some detail,

84
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:55,000
because one of the prime reasons, I believe at least,

85
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:59,000
that this has happened is to be able to process information

86
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:03,520
about depth better than it can be done by rabbits

87
00:05:03,520 --> 00:05:04,680
and other animals.

88
00:05:04,680 --> 00:05:06,960
And so when we talk about depth perception,

89
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:10,720
we are going to deal with this issue in more detail.

90
00:05:10,720 --> 00:05:16,080
Now, if you look at an animal like a rabbit, what you find

91
00:05:16,080 --> 00:05:22,440
and also animals, which, as I've mentioned already,

92
00:05:22,440 --> 00:05:26,360
sorry, I'm going backwards here, that you also

93
00:05:26,360 --> 00:05:27,960
have an efficient amphibia.

94
00:05:27,960 --> 00:05:30,160
You have these sideways-looking eyes.

95
00:05:30,160 --> 00:05:34,880
And now we can ask the question, how do these two eyes

96
00:05:34,880 --> 00:05:37,720
make their connections through the ganglion cells

97
00:05:37,720 --> 00:05:40,640
to the central nervous system?

98
00:05:40,640 --> 00:05:43,480
And that, in fact, was quite an issue at one time,

99
00:05:43,480 --> 00:05:47,000
believe it or not, before anatomical techniques

100
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:49,640
became more sophisticated.

101
00:05:49,640 --> 00:05:52,080
And the reason it became an issue is

102
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:57,200
because it was discovered predominantly actually

103
00:05:57,200 --> 00:06:00,920
by Kahal, who I have mentioned had received the Nobel Prize

104
00:06:00,920 --> 00:06:04,760
for the beautiful work he had done on not only the vision,

105
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:11,720
but in general about the nervous system in 1906 with Golgi.

106
00:06:11,720 --> 00:06:14,960
He discovered, as did several other investigators,

107
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:20,240
that the optic nerve crosses over at the so-called chiasm.

108
00:06:20,240 --> 00:06:22,840
And so the input from the left eye

109
00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:25,480
projects into the right half of the brain

110
00:06:25,480 --> 00:06:28,160
and from the right eye to the left half of the brain.

111
00:06:28,160 --> 00:06:29,920
And that became a huge issue.

112
00:06:29,920 --> 00:06:32,520
Why on earth would this happen?

113
00:06:32,520 --> 00:06:36,280
And we are going to deal with this repeatedly.

114
00:06:36,280 --> 00:06:39,760
It's a very interesting issue.

115
00:06:39,760 --> 00:06:43,400
And even more interesting is the fact

116
00:06:43,400 --> 00:06:47,080
that when the eyes move to the front, what happened

117
00:06:47,080 --> 00:06:52,640
is that this kind of connection became much more complicated.

118
00:06:52,640 --> 00:06:55,880
But before I do that, let me just say a few more words here.

119
00:06:55,880 --> 00:06:59,360
First of all, that in fission amphibia,

120
00:06:59,360 --> 00:07:03,160
you have a huge structure called the optic tectum, which

121
00:07:03,160 --> 00:07:10,440
is the primary visual processing center in the brain.

122
00:07:10,440 --> 00:07:13,960
And there is also the lateral genital nucleus

123
00:07:13,960 --> 00:07:15,920
of the thalamus in these animals,

124
00:07:15,920 --> 00:07:19,600
but that's quite small and not very well developed.

125
00:07:19,600 --> 00:07:23,120
And there is a cortex in these animals,

126
00:07:23,120 --> 00:07:25,880
but it is, again, a primitive area

127
00:07:25,880 --> 00:07:28,640
compared to primates, for example.

128
00:07:28,640 --> 00:07:31,880
And the lateral genital nucleus projects to the cortex.

129
00:07:31,880 --> 00:07:34,160
So the left cortex in these animals

130
00:07:34,160 --> 00:07:36,000
gets input from the right eye.

131
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:39,200
And the obverse is the case for the right cortex.

132
00:07:39,200 --> 00:07:41,400
And so consequently, this cortex

133
00:07:41,400 --> 00:07:43,120
sees this part of the visual field

134
00:07:43,120 --> 00:07:46,160
and this cortex sees this part of the visual field.

135
00:07:46,160 --> 00:07:49,360
It's almost like an inversion.

136
00:07:49,360 --> 00:07:53,560
Now, we come to what happened as a result of the eyes

137
00:07:53,560 --> 00:07:55,400
moving to the front.

138
00:07:55,440 --> 00:07:59,240
And the eyes move to the front in the course of evolution

139
00:07:59,240 --> 00:08:04,800
that necessitated a major rewiring in the visual system.

140
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:08,360
So that's what we are going to look at next, because that's

141
00:08:08,360 --> 00:08:10,040
us, all right?

142
00:08:10,040 --> 00:08:13,520
So what we have here then, we have the two eyes

143
00:08:13,520 --> 00:08:14,960
looking to the front.

144
00:08:14,960 --> 00:08:17,440
And imagine that these two eyes now looking at something.

145
00:08:17,440 --> 00:08:18,840
There's a dot here.

146
00:08:18,840 --> 00:08:21,280
And both eyes are looking at the same dot

147
00:08:21,280 --> 00:08:24,280
so that you only see a single dot.

148
00:08:24,280 --> 00:08:27,400
So now what happens is really something surprising.

149
00:08:27,400 --> 00:08:33,520
What happens is that the, let me interrupt for a second

150
00:08:33,520 --> 00:08:37,080
and tell you that imagine that you cut your eye,

151
00:08:37,080 --> 00:08:39,400
I mean, don't do it, but imagine that you

152
00:08:39,400 --> 00:08:43,160
cut your eye vertically in half, each eye, OK?

153
00:08:43,160 --> 00:08:46,560
And that means that each eye has, if you will,

154
00:08:46,560 --> 00:08:50,240
a nasal half and a temporal half, all right?

155
00:08:50,240 --> 00:08:51,960
So here we have the two eyes.

156
00:08:51,960 --> 00:08:53,400
Here's the left eye.

157
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:59,960
And what we have is a nasal half projects across.

158
00:08:59,960 --> 00:09:04,400
And the same thing is true for the right eye, projects across.

159
00:09:04,400 --> 00:09:07,600
But now if you look at the temporal hemiratne,

160
00:09:07,600 --> 00:09:10,680
hemiratne, remember, that's the word for it,

161
00:09:10,680 --> 00:09:12,800
something very interesting happens.

162
00:09:12,800 --> 00:09:16,200
The temporal hemiratne don't cross over,

163
00:09:16,200 --> 00:09:18,480
but they remain on the same side.

164
00:09:18,480 --> 00:09:23,320
So we have a color code here that the red parts

165
00:09:23,320 --> 00:09:26,760
project across, and the black parts also

166
00:09:26,760 --> 00:09:29,680
project across from one eye, but stay

167
00:09:29,680 --> 00:09:32,280
ipsilateral for the other eye.

168
00:09:32,280 --> 00:09:35,320
So you say, my god, why did this happen?

169
00:09:35,320 --> 00:09:37,840
What on earth is going on?

170
00:09:37,840 --> 00:09:41,520
And then if you study this, you realize

171
00:09:41,520 --> 00:09:47,000
that this is actually one of the few truly logical things

172
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,040
that you encounter when you study the brain.

173
00:09:49,040 --> 00:09:51,600
Many of the things you study in the brain

174
00:09:51,600 --> 00:09:55,840
don't seem to be too logical, because in the force of evolution,

175
00:09:55,840 --> 00:09:59,520
you have to change things in very peculiar subtle ways

176
00:09:59,520 --> 00:10:00,560
to make things work.

177
00:10:00,560 --> 00:10:04,000
You couldn't just redo the whole system from scratch.

178
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:05,840
But this is certainly a logical one,

179
00:10:05,840 --> 00:10:08,680
and I'll explain it to you in just a minute.

180
00:10:08,680 --> 00:10:14,200
OK, so now what we do next is that, again, we

181
00:10:14,200 --> 00:10:16,520
have the two-level genically nuclei,

182
00:10:16,520 --> 00:10:19,080
but now these are in the thalamus.

183
00:10:19,080 --> 00:10:23,040
These two nuclei have grown tremendously in size

184
00:10:23,040 --> 00:10:26,440
and became much more important in visual processing,

185
00:10:26,440 --> 00:10:30,440
as did then the cortex.

186
00:10:30,440 --> 00:10:34,320
This process, by the way, is called encephalization.

187
00:10:34,320 --> 00:10:37,320
So in primitive animals, you have very small cortex,

188
00:10:37,320 --> 00:10:39,320
and then in the course of evolution, the cortex

189
00:10:39,320 --> 00:10:42,040
grew and grew and grew and grew and became

190
00:10:42,040 --> 00:10:44,760
a more and more important structure

191
00:10:44,760 --> 00:10:48,720
in being able to analyze just about anything

192
00:10:48,720 --> 00:10:51,120
and certainly analyze vision.

193
00:10:51,120 --> 00:10:55,400
Now here we have a cortical area in the posterior part

194
00:10:55,400 --> 00:10:58,720
of the brain, as I pointed out to you the last time.

195
00:10:58,720 --> 00:11:01,800
And what we have is a whole series of visual areas

196
00:11:01,800 --> 00:11:04,200
in the cortex.

197
00:11:04,200 --> 00:11:07,240
Most central one, I guess, would be area V1,

198
00:11:07,240 --> 00:11:10,240
the primary so-called primary visual cortex,

199
00:11:10,240 --> 00:11:14,080
to which the lateral genically it projects most profusely.

200
00:11:14,080 --> 00:11:18,200
Then we have a whole bunch of other visual areas,

201
00:11:18,200 --> 00:11:21,720
as we'll discuss in more detail later on.

202
00:11:21,720 --> 00:11:26,520
And then what happens is that we have several other cortical

203
00:11:26,520 --> 00:11:27,080
areas.

204
00:11:27,080 --> 00:11:29,360
The cortex, I think all of you know that,

205
00:11:29,360 --> 00:11:35,200
is divided into four lobes, the frontal, temporal, parietal,

206
00:11:35,200 --> 00:11:38,820
and the rear one is called the occipital lobes.

207
00:11:38,820 --> 00:11:42,760
Now the visual areas that you see in the back here

208
00:11:42,760 --> 00:11:46,200
make extensive interconnections and connections

209
00:11:46,200 --> 00:11:50,080
to these other lobes in the brain

210
00:11:50,080 --> 00:11:53,400
to be able to analyze the visual scene.

211
00:11:53,400 --> 00:11:56,880
Now in addition, what we have still,

212
00:11:56,880 --> 00:11:58,600
as we had in more primitive animals,

213
00:11:58,600 --> 00:12:01,360
we have a superior colliculus, actually two of them,

214
00:12:01,360 --> 00:12:02,520
one on each side.

215
00:12:02,520 --> 00:12:05,000
And then we have another set of areas

216
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:08,280
called the terminal nuclei, the nucleus of the optic tract,

217
00:12:08,280 --> 00:12:12,440
about which we'll just talk a little bit,

218
00:12:12,440 --> 00:12:16,200
because they are specializing in certain visual functions,

219
00:12:16,200 --> 00:12:19,560
but not nearly as compelling and interesting

220
00:12:19,560 --> 00:12:24,080
as the work that has been done in the cortical areas

221
00:12:24,080 --> 00:12:25,920
and in the superior colliculi.

222
00:12:25,920 --> 00:12:27,520
So this is the arrangement.

223
00:12:27,520 --> 00:12:29,400
But now we want to understand, why

224
00:12:29,400 --> 00:12:32,720
do we have this strange connection here?

225
00:12:32,720 --> 00:12:37,480
So to understand that, what we are going to talk about

226
00:12:37,480 --> 00:12:41,960
is the so-called choropter or the Wieth-Miller circle.

227
00:12:41,960 --> 00:12:42,840
Now what does that?

228
00:12:42,840 --> 00:12:46,000
Some clever guy came up with this observation.

229
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:50,680
They made a circle that goes through the nodal point of the eye,

230
00:12:50,680 --> 00:12:54,920
and its diameter depends on where you are fixating.

231
00:12:54,920 --> 00:12:57,480
So fixating here, all right?

232
00:12:57,480 --> 00:13:01,760
And so in this case, the red area,

233
00:13:01,760 --> 00:13:04,880
which is your left visual hemifield,

234
00:13:04,880 --> 00:13:08,680
impinges on the nasal retina of the left eye

235
00:13:08,680 --> 00:13:10,840
and the temporal retina of the other eye.

236
00:13:10,840 --> 00:13:14,360
So if you have an object here, all right,

237
00:13:14,360 --> 00:13:20,120
that hits corresponding retinal elements in the two eyes.

238
00:13:20,120 --> 00:13:21,920
And that is the basic rule.

239
00:13:21,920 --> 00:13:26,520
Anywhere along this choropter, a spot

240
00:13:26,520 --> 00:13:31,000
will activate corresponding regions in the two eyes.

241
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:35,000
And then what happens is that those corresponding points

242
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:39,400
go to the same location in the lateral geniculate nucleus

243
00:13:39,400 --> 00:13:42,760
and the superior coliculus and in the visual cortex.

244
00:13:42,760 --> 00:13:44,800
So the logic of this arrangement

245
00:13:44,800 --> 00:13:49,080
is to be able to have the two eyes have

246
00:13:49,080 --> 00:13:53,120
inputs to corresponding points in the brain.

247
00:13:53,120 --> 00:13:58,000
That way you can see something uniform and clear

248
00:13:58,000 --> 00:13:59,680
rather than seeing double.

249
00:13:59,680 --> 00:14:03,760
So that is then the arrangement that you have in primates.

250
00:14:03,760 --> 00:14:06,400
Now just to anticipate a little bit,

251
00:14:06,400 --> 00:14:09,520
whenever images fall inside or outside the circle,

252
00:14:09,520 --> 00:14:12,000
they do fall on non-correspondent points.

253
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:13,320
And that will have something to do.

254
00:14:13,320 --> 00:14:16,920
And I keep you just curious about that for the time being

255
00:14:16,920 --> 00:14:20,640
about how we process depth.

256
00:14:20,640 --> 00:14:25,040
And that is the mechanism that is involved.

257
00:14:25,040 --> 00:14:26,200
It's called stereopsis.

258
00:14:26,200 --> 00:14:28,680
I've mentioned that very briefly the last time.

259
00:14:28,680 --> 00:14:34,720
OK, so that then summarizes the basic connections

260
00:14:34,720 --> 00:14:35,840
of the system.

261
00:14:35,840 --> 00:14:37,840
And then what we are going to do next

262
00:14:37,840 --> 00:14:39,440
is we're going to go to the retina

263
00:14:39,440 --> 00:14:43,800
and look at it in more details about how the retina is made.

264
00:14:43,800 --> 00:14:47,760
And then we will go in successive steps to higher areas,

265
00:14:47,760 --> 00:14:49,880
sometimes going back and forth, to try

266
00:14:49,880 --> 00:14:53,560
to understand visual processing in general.

267
00:14:53,560 --> 00:14:58,280
All right, so now let's begin by looking at the eye.

268
00:14:58,280 --> 00:15:04,440
Now I don't expect you to know all these names here.

269
00:15:04,440 --> 00:15:07,680
But you should remember, of course, the lens and the cornea.

270
00:15:07,680 --> 00:15:09,040
I know that already.

271
00:15:09,040 --> 00:15:10,480
You know the iris, right?

272
00:15:10,480 --> 00:15:11,120
What's the iris?

273
00:15:13,480 --> 00:15:21,080
OK, now what do we base our views on the color of the eye?

274
00:15:21,080 --> 00:15:27,080
When you say somebody has blue eyes or somebody has brown eyes,

275
00:15:27,080 --> 00:15:32,360
it's based on the fact of what the coloring is of the iris.

276
00:15:32,360 --> 00:15:33,560
Now what's the iris?

277
00:15:33,560 --> 00:15:39,320
The iris can get bigger and smaller

278
00:15:39,320 --> 00:15:44,800
to control the size of the opening into the lens.

279
00:15:44,800 --> 00:15:46,080
All right?

280
00:15:46,080 --> 00:15:50,040
It's much like what you have in cameras

281
00:15:50,040 --> 00:15:53,760
where you have the so-called f-stop, right?

282
00:15:53,760 --> 00:15:58,640
So if you have a large number, say f-16 in a camera,

283
00:15:58,640 --> 00:16:01,960
that is set to 16 if you do it manually

284
00:16:01,960 --> 00:16:03,480
or when it's bright out there.

285
00:16:03,480 --> 00:16:07,440
But when it's dim out there, like we have in this camera here,

286
00:16:07,440 --> 00:16:11,160
you have the lens wide open because it's pretty dark in here.

287
00:16:11,160 --> 00:16:17,840
Then you have a low f-stop, like an f-4 or even lower,

288
00:16:17,840 --> 00:16:22,560
to allow more light to come in, to more photons to enter the eye.

289
00:16:22,560 --> 00:16:25,640
Now, next is here's the lens.

290
00:16:25,640 --> 00:16:29,160
And the fascinating thing about the lens

291
00:16:29,160 --> 00:16:32,600
is that in the eye, the lens is totally different

292
00:16:32,600 --> 00:16:37,440
from the way it is in a camera.

293
00:16:37,440 --> 00:16:40,760
Now, maybe by now, many of you don't even

294
00:16:40,760 --> 00:16:43,000
know the details about a camera.

295
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,240
But one of the details, of course,

296
00:16:45,240 --> 00:16:53,720
is that to bring an image into focus,

297
00:16:53,720 --> 00:16:57,360
you have to adjust the distance of the lens

298
00:16:57,360 --> 00:17:02,120
from the film onto which the image projects.

299
00:17:02,120 --> 00:17:06,640
So the closer an image, the further out the lens has to go.

300
00:17:06,640 --> 00:17:10,880
Now, if that were the case in animals and humans,

301
00:17:10,880 --> 00:17:12,680
you would have a super bulging eye.

302
00:17:12,680 --> 00:17:14,200
So when you look at something closely,

303
00:17:14,200 --> 00:17:15,680
the eye would go out like that.

304
00:17:15,680 --> 00:17:17,520
And that would be really disturbing.

305
00:17:17,520 --> 00:17:23,880
So that is not the case because what happened instead

306
00:17:23,880 --> 00:17:28,800
is something very clever that you don't see in cameras, which

307
00:17:28,800 --> 00:17:33,120
is that you have this lens here, which is, at least

308
00:17:33,120 --> 00:17:42,760
in younger people, is an organ that can vary in thickness.

309
00:17:42,760 --> 00:17:49,160
So you have here some muscles, the ciliary muscles, which

310
00:17:49,160 --> 00:17:53,720
can adjust the thickness of this lens

311
00:17:53,720 --> 00:17:58,680
to allow it to focus properly on the retina.

312
00:17:58,680 --> 00:18:01,720
Now, here the retina is going all around.

313
00:18:01,720 --> 00:18:04,440
It's against the inner wall of the eye, as you all know that,

314
00:18:04,440 --> 00:18:06,360
of course.

315
00:18:06,360 --> 00:18:11,800
And to explain how this is done then very crudely,

316
00:18:11,800 --> 00:18:12,680
here we have it.

317
00:18:12,680 --> 00:18:14,800
If you're an object really near, you

318
00:18:14,800 --> 00:18:18,360
want the lens to be thick to properly focus the image

319
00:18:18,360 --> 00:18:20,080
on the retina.

320
00:18:20,080 --> 00:18:22,720
And if the image is far, you have

321
00:18:22,720 --> 00:18:30,440
to thin the lens to get the proper focus on the retina.

322
00:18:30,440 --> 00:18:33,920
So that's how the lens works, at least

323
00:18:33,920 --> 00:18:35,120
when you're reasonably young.

324
00:18:35,120 --> 00:18:38,120
But then when you get older, what happens

325
00:18:38,120 --> 00:18:46,480
is that the lens becomes progressively stiffer and stiffer.

326
00:18:46,480 --> 00:18:50,440
And because of that, you have to start wearing glasses.

327
00:18:50,440 --> 00:18:56,280
Now, in the olden days, they used to have lenses which came

328
00:18:56,280 --> 00:18:59,120
in two parts, bifocals.

329
00:18:59,120 --> 00:19:00,400
So if you look in the upper part,

330
00:19:00,400 --> 00:19:01,520
you could look in the distance.

331
00:19:01,520 --> 00:19:04,320
If you look in the lower part, you could look close.

332
00:19:04,320 --> 00:19:07,240
Nowadays, they have graduated lenses.

333
00:19:07,240 --> 00:19:10,280
So if you look in the upper part, you can look far.

334
00:19:10,280 --> 00:19:11,600
And if you want to look something,

335
00:19:11,600 --> 00:19:13,880
you're going to look down below.

336
00:19:13,880 --> 00:19:17,200
And then you can see that in focus.

337
00:19:17,200 --> 00:19:20,000
So once you get older, these lenses

338
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,960
don't adjust as much, still a little bit.

339
00:19:22,960 --> 00:19:28,880
But the proof that this is not that important, thank God,

340
00:19:28,880 --> 00:19:34,440
now that we have these graded lenses and we have bifocals,

341
00:19:34,440 --> 00:19:39,600
is that there are a lot of people in old age who develop,

342
00:19:39,600 --> 00:19:41,600
and sometimes not even in old age.

343
00:19:41,600 --> 00:19:44,560
In India, for example, there are a lot

344
00:19:44,560 --> 00:19:47,840
of young kids who have cataracts.

345
00:19:47,840 --> 00:19:48,960
Now, what is cataracts?

346
00:19:48,960 --> 00:19:54,400
Cataracts interferes with the processing of vision

347
00:19:54,400 --> 00:19:56,840
through the lens system.

348
00:19:56,840 --> 00:20:01,880
Things become unclear because all kinds of blockage

349
00:20:01,880 --> 00:20:04,000
occur in the lens itself.

350
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,680
So how is that corrected?

351
00:20:06,680 --> 00:20:09,120
The way that's corrected nowadays,

352
00:20:09,120 --> 00:20:13,320
that you have what is called cataract surgery.

353
00:20:13,320 --> 00:20:13,760
Got it?

354
00:20:13,760 --> 00:20:16,240
Cataract surgery.

355
00:20:16,240 --> 00:20:19,880
So what they do then is they actually, literally,

356
00:20:19,880 --> 00:20:23,560
remove the lens from the eye.

357
00:20:23,560 --> 00:20:30,640
And they put in a fixed lens made out of plastic or glass.

358
00:20:30,640 --> 00:20:34,280
And then, obviously, since there's no adjustment,

359
00:20:34,280 --> 00:20:38,680
you do have to wear glasses to look at things far away

360
00:20:38,680 --> 00:20:40,240
and look at things close.

361
00:20:40,240 --> 00:20:44,640
And most of the time, those would be graduated lenses.

362
00:20:44,640 --> 00:20:48,400
And so people who have cataract surgery

363
00:20:48,400 --> 00:20:52,520
can see extremely well, certainly a lot, lot better

364
00:20:52,520 --> 00:20:57,880
than having to try to deal with lenses that have cataracts.

365
00:20:57,880 --> 00:20:59,840
All right, now, there's one other point

366
00:20:59,840 --> 00:21:03,520
I want to make about the eye here before we move on.

367
00:21:03,520 --> 00:21:04,920
Maybe two points.

368
00:21:04,920 --> 00:21:09,440
One is that about this part of the eye,

369
00:21:09,440 --> 00:21:14,160
that you have here your so-called iris.

370
00:21:14,160 --> 00:21:18,560
And what do you call what's in the center of the iris?

371
00:21:18,560 --> 00:21:20,160
Pupil, very good.

372
00:21:20,160 --> 00:21:23,280
Now, what is the color of the pupil?

373
00:21:26,280 --> 00:21:29,240
In most people, it's black, right?

374
00:21:29,240 --> 00:21:30,160
Why is it black?

375
00:21:30,160 --> 00:21:32,760
How come the pupil is black?

376
00:21:32,760 --> 00:21:33,800
Any thoughts about that?

377
00:21:35,920 --> 00:21:37,960
OK, well, let me tell you.

378
00:21:37,960 --> 00:21:42,520
And I think what I'll do is actually I'll move on.

379
00:21:42,520 --> 00:21:47,440
First, I'll leave this unanswered for just a few minutes.

380
00:21:47,440 --> 00:21:50,440
This I've shown you before, which

381
00:21:50,440 --> 00:21:53,320
is another amazing thing about the eye,

382
00:21:53,320 --> 00:21:57,160
is, namely, that in the fovea, you

383
00:21:57,160 --> 00:22:03,520
have something like 200,000 cones per square millimeter.

384
00:22:03,520 --> 00:22:05,960
200,000.

385
00:22:05,960 --> 00:22:11,080
As you go 5 degrees out, it reduces 10 fold to 20,000.

386
00:22:11,080 --> 00:22:15,200
And then you go 10 degrees, it reduces by half.

387
00:22:15,200 --> 00:22:18,200
So because of this very high density,

388
00:22:18,200 --> 00:22:22,200
I've mentioned this before, in the fovea,

389
00:22:22,200 --> 00:22:24,560
you have high acuity.

390
00:22:24,560 --> 00:22:26,160
And that is one of the prime reasons

391
00:22:26,160 --> 00:22:28,440
you have to move your eye around so that you

392
00:22:28,440 --> 00:22:31,360
can see things in fine detail.

393
00:22:31,360 --> 00:22:36,480
All right, so now let's take another step here

394
00:22:36,480 --> 00:22:40,360
and look at the retina itself.

395
00:22:40,360 --> 00:22:42,920
And let me, first of all, point out to you

396
00:22:42,920 --> 00:22:47,920
I have reversed the image here to have it right side up.

397
00:22:47,920 --> 00:22:49,840
The light actually comes in from the bottom,

398
00:22:49,840 --> 00:22:54,800
goes through all these cells until it hits the photoreceptors.

399
00:22:54,800 --> 00:22:56,360
Here are the photoreceptors.

400
00:22:56,360 --> 00:22:59,400
And at the closest point to the eye,

401
00:22:59,400 --> 00:23:05,760
what we have is called the pigment epithelium.

402
00:23:05,760 --> 00:23:08,400
OK, so what's the pigment epithelium?

403
00:23:08,400 --> 00:23:12,600
The pigment epithelium is a single layer of cells

404
00:23:12,600 --> 00:23:16,240
that are pigmented to such a degree

405
00:23:16,240 --> 00:23:19,240
that when photons come into the eye

406
00:23:19,240 --> 00:23:24,020
and get to the pigment epithelium,

407
00:23:24,020 --> 00:23:28,560
they get absorbed just like a black surface

408
00:23:28,560 --> 00:23:33,120
that you see as a black surface absorbs the photons

409
00:23:33,120 --> 00:23:35,320
and doesn't reflect them.

410
00:23:35,320 --> 00:23:41,000
So because of that, there is no reflection back out.

411
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:48,520
And that explains why the pupil looks black, OK?

412
00:23:48,520 --> 00:23:51,880
Because the light that comes into the eye

413
00:23:51,880 --> 00:23:55,080
doesn't reflect back out.

414
00:23:55,080 --> 00:23:57,120
Now there's a good reason for that,

415
00:23:57,120 --> 00:24:01,120
because if it were to reflect back out, it would scatter

416
00:24:01,120 --> 00:24:07,160
and it would activate many more photoreceptors than it should.

417
00:24:07,160 --> 00:24:09,800
And because of that, you get blurred vision.

418
00:24:09,800 --> 00:24:11,840
Now how do we know this?

419
00:24:11,840 --> 00:24:13,760
All right.

420
00:24:13,760 --> 00:24:20,320
Can you think of any one or any species of animal

421
00:24:20,320 --> 00:24:24,320
where the pigment epithelium is not black?

422
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:29,360
OK, in cat.

423
00:24:29,360 --> 00:24:32,560
OK, so there are some animals.

424
00:24:32,560 --> 00:24:35,080
You see mostly in animals that specialize

425
00:24:35,080 --> 00:24:42,000
in nocturnal vision that have a pigment epithelium which

426
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:47,240
actually has purposefully reflecting molecules in it.

427
00:24:47,240 --> 00:24:51,360
For them, this is called actually the terpetum.

428
00:24:51,360 --> 00:24:57,080
And the purpose of that is to improve your ability

429
00:24:57,080 --> 00:25:00,920
to see so that the photons activate the photoreceptors

430
00:25:00,920 --> 00:25:05,200
not only in one direction, but also when they bounce back.

431
00:25:05,200 --> 00:25:08,680
Now the terpetum is arranged in such a way in most animals

432
00:25:08,680 --> 00:25:10,920
that it doesn't freely scatter the light,

433
00:25:10,920 --> 00:25:14,240
but scatters it in a fairly directional manner.

434
00:25:14,240 --> 00:25:16,880
And because of that, you can see pretty well.

435
00:25:16,880 --> 00:25:21,480
Now, I'm sure that many of you have noticed

436
00:25:21,480 --> 00:25:28,160
that when you drive at night in a deserted area

437
00:25:28,160 --> 00:25:32,800
in the United States, which are decreasing, of course,

438
00:25:32,800 --> 00:25:39,840
you can see sometimes deer on the highway or rabbits,

439
00:25:39,840 --> 00:25:41,240
for that matter.

440
00:25:41,240 --> 00:25:43,880
And when you see them, it looks like they

441
00:25:43,880 --> 00:25:49,040
have two flashlights projecting at you.

442
00:25:49,040 --> 00:25:53,160
It's almost scary, but it also helps you

443
00:25:53,160 --> 00:25:56,440
by being able to avoid hitting them.

444
00:25:56,440 --> 00:26:01,000
Now that's because they have that reflecting terpetum.

445
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:03,680
Let's look at a different example.

446
00:26:03,680 --> 00:26:08,480
What other human can you think of who

447
00:26:08,480 --> 00:26:12,640
does not have a black pigment epithelium?

448
00:26:12,640 --> 00:26:16,200
Or, for that matter, some animals as well, yes?

449
00:26:16,200 --> 00:26:17,160
Very good.

450
00:26:17,160 --> 00:26:18,680
Albinos.

451
00:26:18,680 --> 00:26:19,920
Now what's albinos?

452
00:26:19,920 --> 00:26:23,640
Albinos are individuals or animals

453
00:26:23,640 --> 00:26:30,760
who lack pigments in their skin and, of course,

454
00:26:30,760 --> 00:26:32,600
in their retinae.

455
00:26:32,600 --> 00:26:39,840
So if you look at the eye of an albino,

456
00:26:39,840 --> 00:26:42,440
actually their pupil is not black.

457
00:26:42,440 --> 00:26:46,280
Their pupil is reddish.

458
00:26:46,280 --> 00:26:47,000
Very good.

459
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:48,600
Why is it reddish?

460
00:26:48,600 --> 00:26:59,280
Because you have thousands of, in the retinae rightal law,

461
00:26:59,280 --> 00:27:03,320
especially mostly on the outside here as well,

462
00:27:03,320 --> 00:27:06,760
you have blood vessels.

463
00:27:06,760 --> 00:27:10,200
And the blood vessels, since they carry blood,

464
00:27:10,200 --> 00:27:13,760
when light reflects from them, they will have a reddish color

465
00:27:13,760 --> 00:27:20,640
to them, and thereby endow, if you will, the pupil

466
00:27:20,640 --> 00:27:23,120
with a reddish color.

467
00:27:23,120 --> 00:27:27,840
Now the proof that the main function of the pigment

468
00:27:27,840 --> 00:27:30,960
epithelium is to absorb the incoming light

469
00:27:30,960 --> 00:27:33,760
to prevent scattering is in the fact

470
00:27:33,760 --> 00:27:38,000
that albinos that lack this pigment epithelium

471
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:41,000
have very poor vision.

472
00:27:41,000 --> 00:27:45,520
They often have vision to 20 over 200 or something,

473
00:27:45,520 --> 00:27:51,160
because the light scatters from the epithelium, which

474
00:27:51,160 --> 00:27:54,080
is no longer pigmented in these people,

475
00:27:54,080 --> 00:27:58,400
and activates many photoreceptors,

476
00:27:58,400 --> 00:28:03,960
thereby reducing the ability to see things clearly and sharply.

477
00:28:03,960 --> 00:28:07,960
All right, so now let's go through this retina here.

478
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:13,680
First of all, we have the rods and the cones.

479
00:28:13,680 --> 00:28:18,360
Now this brings me to a very interesting story, at least

480
00:28:18,360 --> 00:28:21,960
for me an interesting story.

481
00:28:21,960 --> 00:28:32,560
Back in the 19th century, there was a person whose name was,

482
00:28:32,560 --> 00:28:36,320
let's see, his name was Max Schulze.

483
00:28:36,320 --> 00:28:38,840
I'm trying to remember when he lived.

484
00:28:38,840 --> 00:28:45,440
He lived from 1825 to 1874.

485
00:28:45,440 --> 00:28:50,720
He developed some new anatomical procedures.

486
00:28:50,720 --> 00:28:57,080
And as I mentioned before, whenever new techniques emerge

487
00:28:57,080 --> 00:29:00,040
or invented, it's likely that you're

488
00:29:00,040 --> 00:29:03,440
going to make some interesting new discoveries.

489
00:29:03,440 --> 00:29:11,320
And so he was the first person by then looking at the retina

490
00:29:11,320 --> 00:29:13,360
and looking at the photoreceptors,

491
00:29:13,360 --> 00:29:18,440
that he said, my god, there are two different kinds of cones.

492
00:29:18,440 --> 00:29:19,280
I shouldn't say cones.

493
00:29:19,280 --> 00:29:21,600
Two different kinds of photoreceptors,

494
00:29:21,600 --> 00:29:26,120
the rods and the cones, all right?

495
00:29:26,120 --> 00:29:29,640
The rods were sort of like the name implies rods,

496
00:29:29,640 --> 00:29:33,360
and the cones were sort of, as the name implies, conish.

497
00:29:33,360 --> 00:29:35,160
Looking, OK?

498
00:29:35,160 --> 00:29:37,560
So he looked at that and looked at that and said, my god,

499
00:29:37,560 --> 00:29:43,480
why on earth would we have in most animals, rods, and cones?

500
00:29:43,480 --> 00:29:44,960
What is their function?

501
00:29:44,960 --> 00:29:46,960
This was a totally new discovery.

502
00:29:46,960 --> 00:29:50,400
And when he first published this, nobody believed him.

503
00:29:50,400 --> 00:29:52,400
They said, oh, they just vary randomly,

504
00:29:52,400 --> 00:29:56,880
and some are more rod-like, some are more cone-like.

505
00:29:56,880 --> 00:30:00,000
But it's just the same photoreceptors.

506
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:02,680
So he, of course, did not accept that.

507
00:30:03,240 --> 00:30:06,000
He saw, by being very careful about it,

508
00:30:06,000 --> 00:30:09,000
that it was two definitely different classes,

509
00:30:09,000 --> 00:30:12,040
just like it is in this picture.

510
00:30:12,040 --> 00:30:14,960
And so he began to ask the question, why on earth

511
00:30:14,960 --> 00:30:16,800
do we have this?

512
00:30:16,800 --> 00:30:20,240
So imagine yourself in the 19th century

513
00:30:20,240 --> 00:30:22,840
coming up with this incredible discovery

514
00:30:22,840 --> 00:30:26,440
and trying to figure out why we have them.

515
00:30:26,440 --> 00:30:28,880
Now, if you're a really good scientist,

516
00:30:28,880 --> 00:30:31,280
then you start to think analytically.

517
00:30:31,280 --> 00:30:33,960
And so what did he do?

518
00:30:33,960 --> 00:30:36,040
I'm so impressed to this day.

519
00:30:36,040 --> 00:30:39,840
He said, well, let me take a closer look at the retina

520
00:30:39,840 --> 00:30:45,280
and see how the rods and the cones are distributed

521
00:30:45,280 --> 00:30:47,040
on the retinal surface.

522
00:30:47,040 --> 00:30:52,840
And he discovered, to go back to the previous slide,

523
00:30:52,840 --> 00:30:57,040
that there is a small region here, the fovea,

524
00:30:57,040 --> 00:30:59,520
where there are only cones.

525
00:30:59,520 --> 00:31:00,840
There are no rods.

526
00:31:00,840 --> 00:31:03,840
I'll now show you a better picture of that in a minute.

527
00:31:03,840 --> 00:31:08,240
And he said, my god, if that's the case,

528
00:31:08,240 --> 00:31:10,720
there must be something different about the way

529
00:31:10,720 --> 00:31:14,560
we see in the fovea than we can see in areas

530
00:31:14,560 --> 00:31:17,600
where we have lots of rods.

531
00:31:17,600 --> 00:31:19,440
So what did he discover?

532
00:31:19,440 --> 00:31:24,600
He discovered that in the fovea,

533
00:31:24,600 --> 00:31:28,800
we don't see too well at night.

534
00:31:28,800 --> 00:31:32,400
And the basis of that, he concluded

535
00:31:32,400 --> 00:31:36,880
that the rods are for night vision.

536
00:31:36,880 --> 00:31:40,320
Now, this was absolutely incredible.

537
00:31:40,320 --> 00:31:43,200
There's only one sad part in this story,

538
00:31:43,200 --> 00:31:51,920
which is that this discovery was made in the early 19th century

539
00:31:51,920 --> 00:31:56,600
before the Nobel Prize was initiated.

540
00:31:56,600 --> 00:31:59,680
Because had the Nobel Prize been initiated

541
00:31:59,680 --> 00:32:02,560
before he made this discovery, there's no question

542
00:32:02,560 --> 00:32:06,400
that he would have received the Nobel Prize for it.

543
00:32:06,400 --> 00:32:09,600
It's such an incredible magnitude of discovery.

544
00:32:09,600 --> 00:32:11,720
All right, so let's go back here, then.

545
00:32:11,720 --> 00:32:14,040
And I pointed out the rods and the cones

546
00:32:14,040 --> 00:32:15,640
and the pigment epithelium.

547
00:32:15,640 --> 00:32:17,720
Now, let's go through here.

548
00:32:17,720 --> 00:32:20,200
And I'll tell you about the rest of the elements.

549
00:32:20,200 --> 00:32:24,800
And then, this time and also in several subsequent sessions,

550
00:32:24,800 --> 00:32:27,200
we will talk about some of these elements

551
00:32:27,200 --> 00:32:32,240
in much more detail, especially about the retinal ganglion

552
00:32:32,240 --> 00:32:32,800
cells.

553
00:32:32,800 --> 00:32:37,440
So anyway, when you come here to the next stage here,

554
00:32:37,440 --> 00:32:41,240
we have the so-called horizontal cells and bipolar cells.

555
00:32:41,240 --> 00:32:43,760
It's been established that the photoreceptors

556
00:32:43,760 --> 00:32:47,000
connect with both of these.

557
00:32:47,000 --> 00:32:50,360
And then, the bipolar cells connect

558
00:32:50,360 --> 00:32:52,320
into the inner portion of the retina,

559
00:32:52,320 --> 00:32:54,240
just as I'll show you in a minute.

560
00:32:54,240 --> 00:32:58,520
Now, this region here is called the OPL.

561
00:32:58,520 --> 00:33:02,280
And that's easy to remember, outer plexiform layer.

562
00:33:02,280 --> 00:33:05,360
Now, then, when you get into the deeper parts of the retina,

563
00:33:05,360 --> 00:33:09,480
you have the so-called emercrine cells and ganglion cells.

564
00:33:09,480 --> 00:33:13,320
And they reside in the inner plexiform layer.

565
00:33:13,320 --> 00:33:21,440
Now, it is the ganglion cells that project out of the eye

566
00:33:21,440 --> 00:33:26,320
and then project, as we had shown you,

567
00:33:26,320 --> 00:33:32,360
to various regions in the brain, most notably,

568
00:33:32,360 --> 00:33:34,400
that we're going to talk about today,

569
00:33:34,400 --> 00:33:36,200
the lateral geniculate nucleus.

570
00:33:39,560 --> 00:33:42,240
Now, let me also point out at this stage.

571
00:33:42,240 --> 00:33:42,740
Let me see.

572
00:33:42,740 --> 00:33:43,920
Do I have a picture of that?

573
00:33:44,320 --> 00:33:51,480
I'll point out to you some numbers.

574
00:33:51,480 --> 00:33:56,720
Here is an enlarged, highly simplified view of rods.

575
00:33:56,720 --> 00:34:02,520
Now, the rods are constructed in an incredibly complex way.

576
00:34:02,520 --> 00:34:06,040
They have these individual so-called disks.

577
00:34:06,040 --> 00:34:10,000
And within each of these disks, there are about 1,000 of them.

578
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:12,720
I only have 25 here, I think, of 24.

579
00:34:12,720 --> 00:34:17,080
There are about 1,000 of these in each rod.

580
00:34:17,080 --> 00:34:21,120
And within each of these disks, you

581
00:34:21,120 --> 00:34:24,360
have about 10,000 rhodopsin molecules.

582
00:34:27,320 --> 00:34:30,280
There are several kinds of opcins, so-called.

583
00:34:30,280 --> 00:34:33,080
Rhodopsins are the ones that you see in rods.

584
00:34:33,080 --> 00:34:37,000
Then there are different kinds of opcins in cones.

585
00:34:37,000 --> 00:34:43,520
And these are the photosensitive molecules

586
00:34:43,520 --> 00:34:48,640
in your receptors so that when a photon comes in and connects

587
00:34:48,640 --> 00:34:54,320
with and hits one of these molecules, they change shape.

588
00:34:54,320 --> 00:34:56,480
And the simplest way of thinking about this

589
00:34:56,480 --> 00:35:00,400
is to say that these molecules, these rhodopsin molecules,

590
00:35:00,400 --> 00:35:03,360
come in two different shapes.

591
00:35:03,360 --> 00:35:09,840
And these two different shapes are what we call very simply

592
00:35:09,840 --> 00:35:16,320
those that are open and those that are sort of closed,

593
00:35:16,320 --> 00:35:19,040
if you'll be describing the molecular shape.

594
00:35:19,040 --> 00:35:21,280
And the easiest way to think of them

595
00:35:21,280 --> 00:35:23,920
is that they are bleached or unbleached.

596
00:35:23,920 --> 00:35:27,800
And so there are millions of these molecules.

597
00:35:27,800 --> 00:35:33,760
And what is it, 10 million in each rod?

598
00:35:33,760 --> 00:35:37,360
And you know how many rods there are in the average eye?

599
00:35:37,360 --> 00:35:41,840
About 120 million, OK?

600
00:35:41,840 --> 00:35:44,880
And about 5 or 6 million cones.

601
00:35:44,880 --> 00:35:46,880
I mean, you're talking about unbelievable numbers.

602
00:35:46,880 --> 00:35:49,160
It's just hard to comprehend.

603
00:35:49,160 --> 00:35:52,560
But at any rate, the molecules here then

604
00:35:52,560 --> 00:35:57,720
are in two different states, bleached and unbleached.

605
00:35:57,720 --> 00:36:01,920
What that means is when a molecule shifts from being

606
00:36:01,920 --> 00:36:06,360
unbleached to bleached as a result of a photon hitting it,

607
00:36:06,360 --> 00:36:14,000
that changes the overall, I'm talking about, of course,

608
00:36:14,000 --> 00:36:18,520
hundreds of molecules typically, the overall degree

609
00:36:18,520 --> 00:36:22,080
of de- and hyperpolarization of the cell.

610
00:36:22,080 --> 00:36:25,200
And that will then determine whether this cell is going

611
00:36:25,200 --> 00:36:27,520
to communicate the next elements below.

612
00:36:27,520 --> 00:36:31,400
And I'll describe that in much more detail in just a minute.

613
00:36:31,400 --> 00:36:35,640
But it gives you an idea of how unbelievably complex this is.

614
00:36:35,640 --> 00:36:37,600
Yet another complexity is that you

615
00:36:37,600 --> 00:36:41,400
have this huge number of rods, OK?

616
00:36:41,400 --> 00:36:45,360
But if you think about it, would these rods

617
00:36:45,360 --> 00:36:51,840
be there in each, sorry, in these rods,

618
00:36:51,840 --> 00:36:57,600
would each of these disks persist for a lifetime?

619
00:36:57,600 --> 00:37:01,440
I know, say the average lifetime is 80 or 90 years.

620
00:37:01,440 --> 00:37:06,240
Would these same guys exist in the eye all the time?

621
00:37:06,240 --> 00:37:07,680
Well, the answer is no.

622
00:37:07,680 --> 00:37:11,320
What happens is there's yet another process, amazingly,

623
00:37:11,320 --> 00:37:18,520
that replaces in steps these disks in the rods.

624
00:37:19,120 --> 00:37:22,280
Usually a few disks have a few days.

625
00:37:22,280 --> 00:37:29,440
So it's a constant dynamic process keeping your rods,

626
00:37:29,440 --> 00:37:33,640
if not your whole body, young, so to speak.

627
00:37:33,640 --> 00:37:34,360
All right.

628
00:37:34,360 --> 00:37:36,480
So anyway, this gives you sort of a crude sense

629
00:37:36,480 --> 00:37:39,560
of a complexity of this system.

630
00:37:39,560 --> 00:37:43,160
But now we are going to shift over and talk

631
00:37:43,840 --> 00:37:51,200
and talk about the retinal ganglion cells.

632
00:37:51,200 --> 00:37:52,600
And then we'll come back and we'll

633
00:37:52,600 --> 00:37:57,000
talk about in a bit more detail about the pre-retinal

634
00:37:57,000 --> 00:37:58,560
ganglionic elements.

635
00:37:58,560 --> 00:38:04,760
Here is a set of pictures created by a wonderful scientist

636
00:38:04,760 --> 00:38:10,280
called Poyak showing you different classes of types,

637
00:38:10,280 --> 00:38:12,920
if you will, of retinal ganglion cells.

638
00:38:12,920 --> 00:38:18,240
The top row here had been named because of the small size

639
00:38:18,240 --> 00:38:24,480
and the small dendritic arbors had been named midget cells.

640
00:38:24,480 --> 00:38:29,680
Whereas these ones below, some of them at least,

641
00:38:29,680 --> 00:38:32,800
are much, much bigger and have much larger dendritic arbors,

642
00:38:32,800 --> 00:38:36,200
as you can see here, have been called parasol cells.

643
00:38:36,200 --> 00:38:38,320
And why they've been called parasol cells

644
00:38:38,320 --> 00:38:41,160
I'll explain to you in just a minute.

645
00:38:41,160 --> 00:38:47,320
Now this set of pictures was created by the Golgi stain

646
00:38:47,320 --> 00:38:53,520
that I mentioned to you before that was invented by Golgi

647
00:38:53,520 --> 00:38:57,760
and extensively studied by Cajal.

648
00:38:57,760 --> 00:38:59,400
And for this incredible work, they

649
00:38:59,400 --> 00:39:02,000
had done a lot of it on the retina.

650
00:39:02,000 --> 00:39:06,600
They received the Nobel Prize in 1906.

651
00:39:06,600 --> 00:39:10,760
So what this disclosed then, an initial view,

652
00:39:10,760 --> 00:39:14,480
was that there are different classes of retinal ganglion

653
00:39:14,480 --> 00:39:15,160
cells.

654
00:39:15,160 --> 00:39:17,440
And so therefore, the big question came up,

655
00:39:17,440 --> 00:39:19,680
why do we have different classes of cells

656
00:39:19,680 --> 00:39:21,680
like the retinal ganglion cells?

657
00:39:21,680 --> 00:39:23,360
What do they do?

658
00:39:23,360 --> 00:39:26,280
Well, initially, some people, again, which is good,

659
00:39:26,280 --> 00:39:29,040
I think one should be resistant to change in new ideas

660
00:39:29,040 --> 00:39:31,160
until they were really proven well.

661
00:39:35,440 --> 00:39:38,600
Some people argued that eyes just continue.

662
00:39:38,600 --> 00:39:42,200
But then it became evident that these

663
00:39:42,200 --> 00:39:44,400
are indeed different classes.

664
00:39:44,400 --> 00:39:48,120
And I will present some evidence to prove that.

665
00:39:48,120 --> 00:39:49,840
So there are actually quite a number

666
00:39:49,840 --> 00:39:52,000
of different classes of retinal ganglion cells

667
00:39:52,000 --> 00:39:53,800
that perform different jobs.

668
00:39:53,800 --> 00:39:55,320
We're going to try to understand what

669
00:39:55,320 --> 00:39:57,600
are the different jobs they perform.

670
00:39:57,600 --> 00:39:59,640
And we're going to concentrate mostly

671
00:39:59,640 --> 00:40:04,400
on these midget and parasol cells

672
00:40:04,400 --> 00:40:08,520
because they by far are the most numerous in the retina.

673
00:40:08,520 --> 00:40:13,200
And make a major, major contribution to all of vision.

674
00:40:13,200 --> 00:40:17,960
All right, so now let's look at the physiology

675
00:40:17,960 --> 00:40:19,560
of retinal ganglion cells.

676
00:40:19,560 --> 00:40:21,800
Now that we know we have these different kinds of ganglion

677
00:40:21,800 --> 00:40:26,040
cells, how do we find out what they do?

678
00:40:26,040 --> 00:40:30,840
Well, what was recognized in the early portions

679
00:40:30,840 --> 00:40:38,480
of the 20th century, that to find out what these cells do

680
00:40:38,480 --> 00:40:42,520
one needs to study their activity.

681
00:40:42,520 --> 00:40:44,800
And to study their activity, what

682
00:40:44,800 --> 00:40:49,640
was developed after many different trials

683
00:40:49,640 --> 00:40:53,960
is to either record from individual axons

684
00:40:53,960 --> 00:40:58,280
or to use a microelectrode that you could put into the eye

685
00:40:58,280 --> 00:41:02,720
and record from individual cells and see what they do.

686
00:41:02,720 --> 00:41:08,120
Well, the first person who did this was Kaffir Hartlein.

687
00:41:08,120 --> 00:41:09,800
And for the beautiful work he had done,

688
00:41:09,800 --> 00:41:13,680
he received the Nobel Prize in 1967.

689
00:41:13,680 --> 00:41:17,640
Now what he did, he was a tremendous surgeon.

690
00:41:17,640 --> 00:41:20,280
And so what he did, he did this in primitive animals

691
00:41:20,280 --> 00:41:25,840
like frogs, he would take the optic nerve

692
00:41:25,840 --> 00:41:30,960
and he would dissect one fiber from the optic nerve,

693
00:41:30,960 --> 00:41:34,280
hook it up, and record electrically from it.

694
00:41:34,280 --> 00:41:36,160
And then what he would do, he would

695
00:41:36,160 --> 00:41:39,920
shine a light into the eye, move it around

696
00:41:39,920 --> 00:41:45,880
until the cell generated action potentials.

697
00:41:45,880 --> 00:41:48,960
That was just a very small region because, as I've mentioned,

698
00:41:48,960 --> 00:41:52,120
each cell in the retina, each ganglion cell,

699
00:41:52,120 --> 00:41:57,000
only sees a teeny, teeny little portion of the visual field.

700
00:41:57,000 --> 00:41:59,480
So that's how he did his experiments.

701
00:41:59,480 --> 00:42:02,440
And he made some really remarkable discoveries

702
00:42:02,440 --> 00:42:05,760
which further then were elaborated

703
00:42:05,760 --> 00:42:07,640
by using slightly different methods.

704
00:42:07,640 --> 00:42:09,320
A slightly different method, first of all,

705
00:42:09,320 --> 00:42:13,080
was to put a microelectrode into the eye.

706
00:42:13,080 --> 00:42:14,960
And I've mentioned that to you before.

707
00:42:14,960 --> 00:42:20,640
The initial microelectrode was a very fine tube of glass

708
00:42:20,640 --> 00:42:23,720
which was heated and then pulled until the tip was only

709
00:42:23,720 --> 00:42:27,400
about a micron in diameter, which enabled you

710
00:42:27,400 --> 00:42:31,240
to record from individual cells in the retina

711
00:42:31,240 --> 00:42:34,280
or for that matter anywhere in the brain.

712
00:42:34,280 --> 00:42:37,000
Now, another change in the methods that emerged

713
00:42:37,000 --> 00:42:44,160
is that instead of shining light directly into the eye,

714
00:42:44,160 --> 00:42:49,560
people use reflected light because in the world, most

715
00:42:49,560 --> 00:42:51,360
of what we see is reflected light.

716
00:42:51,360 --> 00:42:53,440
We don't get, of course, nowadays this

717
00:42:53,440 --> 00:42:57,160
has changed a little bit because we have computers

718
00:42:57,160 --> 00:43:02,920
and we have a TV which was set up in such a way as to mimic

719
00:43:02,920 --> 00:43:05,520
reflected light, if you will.

720
00:43:05,520 --> 00:43:08,480
But in the natural world, for the most part,

721
00:43:08,480 --> 00:43:11,240
we see reflected light.

722
00:43:11,240 --> 00:43:14,080
All right, so when this was done,

723
00:43:14,080 --> 00:43:18,280
this initial work that was done by Keffer Heartline, for which

724
00:43:18,280 --> 00:43:22,720
she received a Nobel Prize, that he discovered

725
00:43:22,720 --> 00:43:27,880
three major classes of cells based on that recording of his,

726
00:43:27,880 --> 00:43:30,520
which he called, first of all, on-cells,

727
00:43:30,520 --> 00:43:35,240
secondly off-cells, and the thirdly, on-off cells.

728
00:43:35,240 --> 00:43:37,360
Now, the on-cells were those which

729
00:43:37,360 --> 00:43:40,960
when you shone light into their so-called receptive field

730
00:43:40,960 --> 00:43:44,040
area, they would discharge vigorously.

731
00:43:44,040 --> 00:43:46,680
You turn the light on here, and you

732
00:43:46,680 --> 00:43:49,800
can see the on-cell fires.

733
00:43:49,800 --> 00:43:52,200
An off-cell, on the other hand, fired

734
00:43:52,200 --> 00:43:57,200
when you turned off the light using this particular method.

735
00:43:57,200 --> 00:44:00,640
And on-off cells were those which fired both to the onset

736
00:44:00,640 --> 00:44:02,680
and the termination of the light.

737
00:44:02,680 --> 00:44:04,760
And that's why they were called and are still

738
00:44:04,760 --> 00:44:08,880
called to this day on and off cells.

739
00:44:08,880 --> 00:44:11,400
So that was an incredible discovery

740
00:44:11,400 --> 00:44:17,720
and has created a major evolution in the study

741
00:44:17,720 --> 00:44:20,800
of the visual system.

742
00:44:20,800 --> 00:44:27,760
All right, so now, doing this kind of work in more detail,

743
00:44:27,760 --> 00:44:33,160
using especially mostly reflected light,

744
00:44:33,160 --> 00:44:37,480
several other major discoveries were made.

745
00:44:37,480 --> 00:44:42,600
A fellow at Harvard in his later years, originally

746
00:44:42,600 --> 00:44:44,960
at Johns Hopkins, did experiments

747
00:44:44,960 --> 00:44:49,480
carefully studying the receptive field structure

748
00:44:49,480 --> 00:44:50,480
of these cells.

749
00:44:50,480 --> 00:44:53,440
He would record, in this case, with microelectrodes

750
00:44:53,440 --> 00:44:57,280
from a single cell, a gamma ganglion cell,

751
00:44:57,280 --> 00:45:00,440
and then see how it responded when you fiddled around

752
00:45:00,440 --> 00:45:03,400
with the light in the receptive field.

753
00:45:03,400 --> 00:45:05,920
So imagine, then, that the eye is fixed.

754
00:45:05,920 --> 00:45:07,040
It's looking out.

755
00:45:07,040 --> 00:45:09,400
You're putting an electrode into, in this case,

756
00:45:09,400 --> 00:45:11,840
into the eye or wherever you put it.

757
00:45:11,840 --> 00:45:15,640
And then you can use lights, maybe like a projector,

758
00:45:15,640 --> 00:45:19,280
move it around to find out where the receptive field is.

759
00:45:19,280 --> 00:45:22,600
And then you can present, say, a small spot of light,

760
00:45:22,600 --> 00:45:25,320
or you make it a big spot, make it different colors,

761
00:45:25,320 --> 00:45:26,680
and so on and so on.

762
00:45:26,680 --> 00:45:30,400
So when this was done, a remarkable discovery

763
00:45:30,400 --> 00:45:36,720
was made by Kufler, namely that the so-called on and off

764
00:45:36,720 --> 00:45:40,640
cells were not just responding to the onset

765
00:45:40,640 --> 00:45:42,400
and termination of light.

766
00:45:42,400 --> 00:45:46,600
Instead, what they did was they responded vigorously

767
00:45:46,600 --> 00:45:50,440
to a small spot in the center of the so-called receptive field.

768
00:45:50,440 --> 00:45:53,440
But if you use a large spot like that,

769
00:45:53,440 --> 00:45:57,000
there was much less of a response.

770
00:45:57,000 --> 00:45:59,440
And that was true also for the off cells

771
00:45:59,440 --> 00:46:01,120
and for the on-off cells.

772
00:46:01,120 --> 00:46:04,320
So the surround we can think of as being inhibitory

773
00:46:04,320 --> 00:46:06,360
with respect to the center.

774
00:46:06,360 --> 00:46:10,960
And so the big question was, why on earth

775
00:46:10,960 --> 00:46:15,080
did this complex arrangement evolve

776
00:46:15,080 --> 00:46:19,280
of having cells that have not only an excitatory center

777
00:46:19,280 --> 00:46:22,440
but also have an inhibitory surround?

778
00:46:22,440 --> 00:46:25,080
And now I'm going to let you think about this for a minute.

779
00:46:25,080 --> 00:46:29,080
And eventually, I will tell you as to why we

780
00:46:29,080 --> 00:46:31,160
have this organization.

781
00:46:31,160 --> 00:46:33,680
Because we are going to devote a whole session

782
00:46:33,680 --> 00:46:38,960
to the on and off systems and also a complete session

783
00:46:38,960 --> 00:46:40,720
to the midget and parasol cells.

784
00:46:40,720 --> 00:46:43,680
And that's when we are going to discuss this in detail.

785
00:46:43,880 --> 00:46:49,240
The important thing, as you learn about these things,

786
00:46:49,240 --> 00:46:52,160
is to be an active thinker.

787
00:46:52,160 --> 00:46:54,760
And if something comes up and say, well, why did this happen?

788
00:46:54,760 --> 00:46:55,600
Why did that happen?

789
00:46:55,600 --> 00:46:56,160
Why is this?

790
00:46:56,160 --> 00:46:56,960
Why is that?

791
00:46:56,960 --> 00:46:58,880
When you actively think about that,

792
00:46:58,880 --> 00:47:00,680
then it becomes interesting.

793
00:47:00,680 --> 00:47:03,480
And also, once the answers come, it

794
00:47:03,480 --> 00:47:07,720
becomes insightful and exciting to understand it.

795
00:47:07,720 --> 00:47:14,760
OK, so now, a series of investigators

796
00:47:14,760 --> 00:47:22,800
did experiments in which they did labeling of just the cell

797
00:47:22,800 --> 00:47:26,880
bodies in the retina and did what is called a hole mount.

798
00:47:26,880 --> 00:47:28,040
What's a hole mount?

799
00:47:28,040 --> 00:47:30,240
You take the retina, you flatten it out,

800
00:47:30,240 --> 00:47:35,040
then you look at this whole layout through a microscope.

801
00:47:35,040 --> 00:47:36,840
And if you label them these cells,

802
00:47:36,840 --> 00:47:39,680
and this was a so-called nistel stain that

803
00:47:39,680 --> 00:47:45,800
stains the cell bodies, but doesn't stain the processes

804
00:47:45,800 --> 00:47:48,800
like the dendrites or the exons, what they found

805
00:47:48,800 --> 00:47:51,480
was that if you did this quantitatively,

806
00:47:51,480 --> 00:47:56,080
they could distinguish three very distinct different

807
00:47:56,080 --> 00:47:58,040
classes of cells.

808
00:47:58,040 --> 00:48:00,040
Turned out later that there were more than three.

809
00:48:00,040 --> 00:48:02,840
But in this case, just doing it crudely like that,

810
00:48:02,840 --> 00:48:04,240
you can see those big cells.

811
00:48:04,240 --> 00:48:06,480
That one, that one, that one, that one, that one,

812
00:48:06,480 --> 00:48:07,800
and so on.

813
00:48:07,800 --> 00:48:10,600
Maybe one out of seven or eight in this sample

814
00:48:10,600 --> 00:48:12,040
are these huge cells.

815
00:48:12,040 --> 00:48:15,000
And then you have some smaller cells,

816
00:48:15,000 --> 00:48:17,640
and then you also have some tiny cells.

817
00:48:17,640 --> 00:48:19,600
And if you did this quantitatively,

818
00:48:19,600 --> 00:48:25,880
they argued that they formed distinct, separate populations.

819
00:48:25,880 --> 00:48:28,520
They were not a continuum.

820
00:48:28,520 --> 00:48:31,160
So then, that, of course, is a basic requirement

821
00:48:31,160 --> 00:48:35,600
to prove that, indeed, these are different classes of cells

822
00:48:35,600 --> 00:48:38,640
that probably perform different jobs.

823
00:48:38,640 --> 00:48:42,520
So what can you do to prove this further?

824
00:48:42,520 --> 00:48:46,400
Well, subsequent to this kind of work,

825
00:48:46,400 --> 00:48:51,040
people began to look at another interesting question

826
00:48:51,040 --> 00:48:55,280
about how these retinal ganglion cells send their signals

827
00:48:55,280 --> 00:48:57,800
to the central nervous system.

828
00:48:57,800 --> 00:48:59,920
And it's been discovered not just in the retina,

829
00:48:59,920 --> 00:49:06,880
but in many other parts of the human and animal body

830
00:49:06,880 --> 00:49:16,160
that the rapidity with which an axon can send its action

831
00:49:16,160 --> 00:49:19,880
potentials down from one location to the next,

832
00:49:19,880 --> 00:49:26,440
from the cell body, say, to the axon terminals,

833
00:49:26,440 --> 00:49:30,680
is very much depends on the size of the cell

834
00:49:30,680 --> 00:49:33,040
and the size of the axon.

835
00:49:33,040 --> 00:49:38,200
So if you have a small cell and a very thin axon,

836
00:49:38,200 --> 00:49:42,240
the conduction is slower than if you have a big cell

837
00:49:42,240 --> 00:49:44,280
and a big axon.

838
00:49:44,280 --> 00:49:47,640
So if that's the case, people argued

839
00:49:47,640 --> 00:49:52,600
that if you were to take the optic nerve

840
00:49:52,600 --> 00:49:57,200
and stimulate it at one site and a little base down,

841
00:49:57,200 --> 00:50:01,600
maybe back towards the retina, record

842
00:50:01,600 --> 00:50:05,680
and see what kind of overall general, not action potentials,

843
00:50:05,680 --> 00:50:08,440
but overall activation takes place.

844
00:50:08,440 --> 00:50:13,200
And when that was done, amazing discovery was made.

845
00:50:13,200 --> 00:50:18,960
In this case, you can see we have two major dips here.

846
00:50:18,960 --> 00:50:20,680
This is time.

847
00:50:20,680 --> 00:50:22,760
Here is electrical stimulation.

848
00:50:22,760 --> 00:50:26,480
Here is the activation of hundreds and hundreds of fibers.

849
00:50:26,480 --> 00:50:29,960
And it shows that they form distinct groups, a very rapidly

850
00:50:29,960 --> 00:50:32,880
conducting group, a slow conducting group,

851
00:50:32,880 --> 00:50:36,480
and a very slow, more diffuse group.

852
00:50:36,480 --> 00:50:41,400
So then it was subsequent, I'll talk about that later,

853
00:50:41,400 --> 00:50:44,800
it was subsequently established that these very rapidly

854
00:50:44,800 --> 00:50:52,720
conducting axons come from the so-called parasol cells.

855
00:50:52,720 --> 00:50:56,400
The medium conducting ones come from the midget cells.

856
00:50:56,400 --> 00:50:57,560
All right?

857
00:50:57,560 --> 00:51:00,680
I'll tell you exactly how that was established.

858
00:51:00,680 --> 00:51:03,520
But now that has become a solid fact.

859
00:51:03,520 --> 00:51:04,160
OK.

860
00:51:04,160 --> 00:51:07,800
So now, if you go back to the anatomy of these cell types,

861
00:51:07,800 --> 00:51:13,920
if you look at them at equivalent distances

862
00:51:13,920 --> 00:51:19,560
of eccentricity, OK, 1, 3, and 5.7 degrees,

863
00:51:19,560 --> 00:51:24,240
here are examples of the midget cell dendritic arbors.

864
00:51:24,240 --> 00:51:25,360
So again, this is a whole mind.

865
00:51:25,360 --> 00:51:29,000
You're looking straight down at the cell, all right?

866
00:51:29,000 --> 00:51:34,320
You can see these are very, very constricted dendritic arbors.

867
00:51:34,320 --> 00:51:36,960
Now, by contrast, the parasol cells

868
00:51:36,960 --> 00:51:41,640
have much, much larger, three times larger in diameter

869
00:51:41,640 --> 00:51:43,360
dendritic arbors.

870
00:51:43,360 --> 00:51:46,480
And now I can tell you, which I deferred before,

871
00:51:46,480 --> 00:51:48,760
is that if you look at these, the reason

872
00:51:48,760 --> 00:51:51,800
they're called parasol cells is what?

873
00:51:51,800 --> 00:51:57,040
Because it looks like an umbrella, OK?

874
00:51:57,040 --> 00:51:59,440
So this looks like an umbrella.

875
00:51:59,440 --> 00:52:01,480
And of course, that's parasol.

876
00:52:01,480 --> 00:52:04,360
So that's why they are called parasol cells.

877
00:52:04,360 --> 00:52:06,520
And these guys, most of us interesting,

878
00:52:06,520 --> 00:52:08,560
are called midget cells because they're midget.

879
00:52:08,560 --> 00:52:09,920
They're small.

880
00:52:09,920 --> 00:52:13,360
All right, so that then established,

881
00:52:13,360 --> 00:52:15,960
this work was done just 20 years ago,

882
00:52:15,960 --> 00:52:18,480
it was established that you have indeed

883
00:52:18,480 --> 00:52:22,280
these two very, very distinct populations of cells.

884
00:52:22,280 --> 00:52:26,680
So now the big question came up, why do we have on and off

885
00:52:26,680 --> 00:52:27,400
cells?

886
00:52:27,400 --> 00:52:29,840
Why do we have midget and parasol cells?

887
00:52:29,840 --> 00:52:32,280
And as I say, we are going to discuss this in considerable

888
00:52:32,280 --> 00:52:33,240
detail.

889
00:52:33,240 --> 00:52:36,400
But let me just give you a crude introduction.

890
00:52:36,400 --> 00:52:39,080
If you look at the parasol cell, obviously,

891
00:52:39,080 --> 00:52:41,400
because of the huge dendritic arbors,

892
00:52:41,400 --> 00:52:46,120
they have large, relatively speaking, much larger receptor

893
00:52:46,120 --> 00:52:49,280
feels three times bigger than midget cells.

894
00:52:49,280 --> 00:52:54,400
The midget cells are so specific that each cell

895
00:52:54,400 --> 00:52:58,120
in central retina, its input for the excitatory center

896
00:52:58,120 --> 00:53:00,920
from just one single cone.

897
00:53:00,920 --> 00:53:03,400
Now, what does that mean?

898
00:53:03,400 --> 00:53:06,520
But I didn't emphasize before, but I'm sure you all

899
00:53:06,520 --> 00:53:10,520
know that we have three major classes of cones, which

900
00:53:10,520 --> 00:53:14,520
some people call red, green, and blue.

901
00:53:14,520 --> 00:53:17,760
And then when you say that, the real aficionados say,

902
00:53:17,760 --> 00:53:19,880
come on, you can't say that.

903
00:53:19,880 --> 00:53:23,480
You're supposed to say short, medium, and long wavelength

904
00:53:23,480 --> 00:53:24,840
sensitive neurons.

905
00:53:24,840 --> 00:53:26,960
But we'll just call them red, green, and blue.

906
00:53:26,960 --> 00:53:32,760
Now, the reason then is that the center of one class of midget

907
00:53:32,760 --> 00:53:34,880
cells gets included only from red cones,

908
00:53:34,880 --> 00:53:37,760
another from green cones, and some from blue cones.

909
00:53:37,760 --> 00:53:39,920
I'll show that in more detail in a minute.

910
00:53:39,920 --> 00:53:43,000
So we have these three subtypes.

911
00:53:43,000 --> 00:53:46,560
And so therefore, we can say that the midget cells,

912
00:53:46,560 --> 00:53:48,400
because they're so teeny, they should

913
00:53:48,400 --> 00:53:50,600
be able to see fine detail.

914
00:53:50,600 --> 00:53:53,600
And because of this wiring, they should

915
00:53:53,600 --> 00:53:55,760
be able to tell you about color.

916
00:53:55,760 --> 00:53:58,720
By contrast, if you look at the parasol cells,

917
00:53:58,720 --> 00:54:03,040
they get a mix of inputs, both in the center and the surround.

918
00:54:03,040 --> 00:54:06,080
So it's unlikely that they can tell you about color.

919
00:54:06,080 --> 00:54:09,000
And we'll examine that in much more detail soon.

920
00:54:09,000 --> 00:54:11,160
All right, so now, the other interesting thing

921
00:54:11,160 --> 00:54:14,960
about this system is it makes you speculate as to why we have

922
00:54:14,960 --> 00:54:17,320
them, and we'll elaborate on that,

923
00:54:17,320 --> 00:54:19,800
is that the midget system, when you turn on the light,

924
00:54:19,800 --> 00:54:23,280
when you shine a small spot in the center receptive field,

925
00:54:23,280 --> 00:54:26,400
gives you a fairly sustained response for the brief time

926
00:54:26,400 --> 00:54:29,400
that was maybe a second or two.

927
00:54:29,400 --> 00:54:32,760
But the parasol system gives you only a burst,

928
00:54:32,760 --> 00:54:36,920
like that, instead of a sustained response.

929
00:54:36,920 --> 00:54:38,200
So think about that.

930
00:54:38,200 --> 00:54:40,760
Say, why would we have two systems which are not

931
00:54:40,760 --> 00:54:44,040
only different in size, but also different in the profile

932
00:54:44,040 --> 00:54:46,240
of their responses?

933
00:54:46,240 --> 00:54:48,840
And then, once you have this question in your mind,

934
00:54:48,840 --> 00:54:52,120
then you'll be eager to find out when we talk about that time

935
00:54:52,120 --> 00:54:53,640
after next.

936
00:54:53,640 --> 00:54:56,520
OK, so now let's go back to the beginning here

937
00:54:56,520 --> 00:54:58,120
and talk about the photoreceptors

938
00:54:58,120 --> 00:55:04,800
in just a bit more detail, so we can get a better picture of it.

939
00:55:04,800 --> 00:55:07,640
Here, as I've mentioned to you before, in the fovea,

940
00:55:07,640 --> 00:55:13,280
you have very, very tightly packed cone photoreceptors.

941
00:55:13,280 --> 00:55:16,000
They're almost hexagonal in shape rather than round.

942
00:55:16,000 --> 00:55:18,120
They're so tightly packed.

943
00:55:18,120 --> 00:55:20,440
But then, when you go five degrees out,

944
00:55:20,440 --> 00:55:25,200
you first of all see that the cones got to be much larger.

945
00:55:25,200 --> 00:55:28,960
And now, you have an intermixture of rods there

946
00:55:28,960 --> 00:55:31,800
that you can see.

947
00:55:31,800 --> 00:55:35,000
So in this region, you can process

948
00:55:35,000 --> 00:55:37,520
both information at low illumination levels

949
00:55:37,520 --> 00:55:40,360
and high illumination levels, whereas here,

950
00:55:40,360 --> 00:55:47,680
as Kaffir Hotline had proven, you can only

951
00:55:47,680 --> 00:55:52,520
see under fairly right normal illumination conditions.

952
00:55:57,040 --> 00:56:01,120
OK, so now, if you go even further out in the retina,

953
00:56:01,120 --> 00:56:03,040
this is a beautiful three-dimensional looking

954
00:56:03,040 --> 00:56:07,120
picture, this shows the rods and the cones in the periphery.

955
00:56:07,120 --> 00:56:09,840
Many, many more rods per unit area,

956
00:56:09,840 --> 00:56:13,520
and the cones have gotten even bigger.

957
00:56:13,520 --> 00:56:17,600
And so obviously, the ability for the cones

958
00:56:17,600 --> 00:56:24,000
to process fine information has been degraded as a result.

959
00:56:24,000 --> 00:56:26,560
Now again, as I've mentioned to you before,

960
00:56:26,560 --> 00:56:31,640
we have in humans, there are about 120 million rods,

961
00:56:31,640 --> 00:56:35,520
now five million cones, in each retina.

962
00:56:35,520 --> 00:56:38,680
OK, so now, if you look at the overall distribution,

963
00:56:38,680 --> 00:56:40,440
this is not too important, you can

964
00:56:40,440 --> 00:56:44,080
see that as you go away from the fovea,

965
00:56:44,080 --> 00:56:48,280
there's a very rapid decline in the number of cones.

966
00:56:48,280 --> 00:56:51,000
That's a hash line.

967
00:56:51,000 --> 00:56:54,920
And then there are no rods in the fovea,

968
00:56:54,920 --> 00:56:57,600
but they increase rapidly, and then they fall off.

969
00:56:57,600 --> 00:57:00,360
So that's the overall distribution.

970
00:57:00,360 --> 00:57:03,440
And therefore, you can see fine detail in the center,

971
00:57:03,440 --> 00:57:08,560
and you're very sensitive for night vision,

972
00:57:08,560 --> 00:57:14,960
and not in a far periphery, but sort of in the mid section

973
00:57:14,960 --> 00:57:21,800
here, anywhere between 20 and 40 degrees from the fovea.

974
00:57:21,800 --> 00:57:25,480
Now let me remind you, because I'm sure you know all this

975
00:57:25,480 --> 00:57:27,000
already, a few facts.

976
00:57:32,480 --> 00:57:34,880
Some of this, I will just write down a couple of these things.

977
00:57:34,880 --> 00:57:42,560
First of all, what is a degree of visual angle?

978
00:57:42,560 --> 00:57:44,680
That's a good question.

979
00:57:44,680 --> 00:57:47,080
What does it mean on the retinal surface?

980
00:57:47,080 --> 00:57:50,640
Just let me give you an interesting mnemonic.

981
00:57:50,640 --> 00:57:53,680
If you stick out your arm like that,

982
00:57:53,680 --> 00:57:56,200
and you look at your thumbnail, that

983
00:57:56,200 --> 00:58:00,720
imprints one degree of visual angle on the retinal surface.

984
00:58:01,440 --> 00:58:03,800
OK?

985
00:58:03,800 --> 00:58:08,880
Now we are going to talk a lot about very small measurements.

986
00:58:08,880 --> 00:58:11,640
And so when we talk about small measurements,

987
00:58:11,640 --> 00:58:14,320
I mentioned already that the tip of a microelectro

988
00:58:14,320 --> 00:58:15,920
is about one micron.

989
00:58:15,920 --> 00:58:18,280
And what is a micron?

990
00:58:18,280 --> 00:58:19,720
Anybody know?

991
00:58:19,720 --> 00:58:20,200
Yes?

992
00:58:20,200 --> 00:58:22,760
10 to the negative 6.

993
00:58:22,760 --> 00:58:24,600
10 to the negative 6.

994
00:58:24,600 --> 00:58:25,560
OK.

995
00:58:25,560 --> 00:58:31,320
One micron is 1,000th of a millimeter.

996
00:58:31,320 --> 00:58:31,820
OK?

997
00:58:34,680 --> 00:58:37,880
Now, talking about that in terms of millimeters, which

998
00:58:37,880 --> 00:58:41,120
you are going to do a lot, that's the greatest convention,

999
00:58:41,120 --> 00:58:46,800
we need to also know what is the relationship between inches

1000
00:58:46,800 --> 00:58:50,240
and millimeters and centimeters.

1001
00:58:50,240 --> 00:58:51,960
OK, so if you take one inch.

1002
00:58:56,560 --> 00:58:59,680
You all know what an inch is, I guess.

1003
00:58:59,680 --> 00:59:01,560
Roughly, if you look at your thumbnail again,

1004
00:59:01,560 --> 00:59:03,800
it's maybe a little bit under an inch.

1005
00:59:03,800 --> 00:59:05,360
OK?

1006
00:59:05,360 --> 00:59:15,240
Now, one inch equals 2.54 millimeters.

1007
00:59:15,240 --> 00:59:16,760
OK?

1008
00:59:16,760 --> 00:59:20,280
So that's a very interesting mnemonic to remember.

1009
00:59:20,280 --> 00:59:23,640
So whenever you look at various things

1010
00:59:23,640 --> 00:59:27,200
and try to understand size, you may

1011
00:59:27,200 --> 00:59:32,200
need to make that conversion, especially if you have always

1012
00:59:32,200 --> 00:59:34,800
thought about things in terms of inches.

1013
00:59:34,800 --> 00:59:35,300
Yes?

1014
00:59:35,300 --> 00:59:37,200
I think you mentored centimeters.

1015
00:59:37,200 --> 00:59:37,700
What?

1016
00:59:37,700 --> 00:59:39,400
Did you mean to write centimeters?

1017
00:59:42,440 --> 00:59:44,520
You're right.

1018
00:59:44,520 --> 00:59:48,080
2.5 centimeters.

1019
00:59:48,080 --> 00:59:51,080
Yeah, 254 millimeters.

1020
00:59:51,080 --> 00:59:51,960
You're right.

1021
00:59:51,960 --> 00:59:53,600
OK, sorry about that.

1022
00:59:53,600 --> 00:59:56,480
Very good, thank you for pointing that out.

1023
00:59:56,480 --> 01:00:00,680
OK, so now, what we are going to do next

1024
01:00:00,680 --> 01:00:02,920
is we're going to move on, and I'll

1025
01:00:02,920 --> 01:00:08,080
tell you another clever experiment that had been done.

1026
01:00:08,080 --> 01:00:10,520
These people here, McCrane and all,

1027
01:00:10,520 --> 01:00:13,720
developed a method which enabled them to selectively

1028
01:00:13,720 --> 01:00:17,560
label the blue cells in the retina.

1029
01:00:17,560 --> 01:00:19,680
And this shows a picture of that.

1030
01:00:19,680 --> 01:00:22,920
All the rest of them are red and green cells.

1031
01:00:23,600 --> 01:00:38,720
So only one out of eight cones is in the foveal area,

1032
01:00:38,720 --> 01:00:39,880
near foveal area.

1033
01:00:39,880 --> 01:00:43,080
I should say this is both the foveal and the periphoveal,

1034
01:00:43,080 --> 01:00:47,360
is black, the rest of them are red and green cones.

1035
01:00:47,720 --> 01:00:50,000
OK?

1036
01:00:50,000 --> 01:00:54,920
Now, let's become specific about the facts here,

1037
01:00:54,920 --> 01:00:58,800
and tell you first of all that one degree, roughly one

1038
01:00:58,800 --> 01:01:02,240
degree, which I pointed out is like a thumb,

1039
01:01:02,240 --> 01:01:09,040
it comprises 200 micro on the retinal surface.

1040
01:01:09,040 --> 01:01:17,200
The inter-cone distance in the foveal is only 2.4 micro,

1041
01:01:17,200 --> 01:01:24,480
whereas per square millimeter, I already mentioned this,

1042
01:01:24,480 --> 01:01:28,840
you have about 200,000 cones.

1043
01:01:28,840 --> 01:01:32,680
You go out five degrees, that reduces 10-fold.

1044
01:01:32,680 --> 01:01:36,080
And then, this I pointed out to you several times,

1045
01:01:36,080 --> 01:01:38,040
that's a good thing to remember.

1046
01:01:38,040 --> 01:01:39,680
And one thing I haven't mentioned before

1047
01:01:39,680 --> 01:01:43,720
is that if you look at a 12-front ladder, I,

1048
01:01:43,720 --> 01:01:47,080
when you look at it at 23 centimeters,

1049
01:01:47,080 --> 01:01:48,880
that activates about 80 cones.

1050
01:01:48,880 --> 01:01:50,960
So that gives you sort of a handle

1051
01:01:50,960 --> 01:01:54,560
on what is the nature of activation there.

1052
01:01:54,560 --> 01:01:56,240
And I already said that several times,

1053
01:01:56,240 --> 01:01:59,520
namely that each rod has 1,000 discs,

1054
01:01:59,520 --> 01:02:05,840
and each disc in it has 10,000 molecules.

1055
01:02:05,840 --> 01:02:06,680
OK.

1056
01:02:06,680 --> 01:02:11,400
And as I've just mentioned, one out of eight cones is blue,

1057
01:02:11,400 --> 01:02:13,600
the other are fairly equally numbered,

1058
01:02:13,600 --> 01:02:16,240
but varies from person to person.

1059
01:02:16,240 --> 01:02:16,680
OK.

1060
01:02:16,680 --> 01:02:27,520
So now, we are going to tell you about yet another experiment.

1061
01:02:27,520 --> 01:02:30,520
And in this, this is an incredibly clever experiment

1062
01:02:30,520 --> 01:02:35,360
that was carried out by Ursula Drager.

1063
01:02:35,360 --> 01:02:37,840
At that time, when she did this beautiful experiment,

1064
01:02:37,840 --> 01:02:40,960
she did this at Harvard, at Harvard Medical School.

1065
01:02:40,960 --> 01:02:43,040
And what she did was this.

1066
01:02:43,040 --> 01:02:49,720
She took the words fiat lux, put it on a screen,

1067
01:02:49,720 --> 01:02:54,400
and exposed it to a dark-adapted animal,

1068
01:02:54,400 --> 01:02:56,880
and the animal being a mouse.

1069
01:02:56,880 --> 01:02:57,760
OK.

1070
01:02:57,760 --> 01:03:00,560
And then, her procedure enabled her

1071
01:03:00,560 --> 01:03:04,760
to label, using a monoclonal antibody label,

1072
01:03:04,760 --> 01:03:09,440
those cells that had been activated by fiat lux.

1073
01:03:09,440 --> 01:03:10,280
OK.

1074
01:03:10,280 --> 01:03:14,080
So this is then what she, this is the actual retina,

1075
01:03:14,080 --> 01:03:18,520
flat-mount retina, showing you what had been activated.

1076
01:03:18,520 --> 01:03:20,960
Now, that's remarkable, beautiful.

1077
01:03:20,960 --> 01:03:26,120
And she came and gave a talk here at MIT maybe about 15

1078
01:03:26,120 --> 01:03:28,840
years ago.

1079
01:03:28,840 --> 01:03:31,720
And when she presented this picture,

1080
01:03:31,720 --> 01:03:36,520
one of the students, it wasn't any of you, I guess,

1081
01:03:36,520 --> 01:03:39,880
raised her hand and was very upset.

1082
01:03:40,880 --> 01:03:43,440
But, but, but, but, but.

1083
01:03:43,440 --> 01:03:46,040
And so Ursula said, yes?

1084
01:03:46,040 --> 01:03:50,000
So what do you think this patient, this subject asked?

1085
01:03:50,000 --> 01:03:53,880
This listener asked.

1086
01:03:53,880 --> 01:03:56,480
Well, this was a very active thinker.

1087
01:03:56,480 --> 01:04:00,320
This person said, but, but, but, was this image

1088
01:04:00,320 --> 01:04:03,000
right set up on the retinal surface?

1089
01:04:03,000 --> 01:04:04,760
That was a question.

1090
01:04:04,760 --> 01:04:08,720
And Ursula Drager said, no, it was upside down.

1091
01:04:08,720 --> 01:04:13,040
I just rotated it 180 degrees so you can see it.

1092
01:04:13,040 --> 01:04:14,960
All right.

1093
01:04:14,960 --> 01:04:21,680
Well, interestingly enough, there were times

1094
01:04:21,680 --> 01:04:23,400
when this became an issue.

1095
01:04:23,400 --> 01:04:27,200
First of all, before, people knew about lenses,

1096
01:04:27,200 --> 01:04:29,440
and that lenses turn images upside down.

1097
01:04:30,400 --> 01:04:39,160
And, and secondly, because many people

1098
01:04:39,160 --> 01:04:42,560
didn't believe that the images are upside on the retina.

1099
01:04:42,560 --> 01:04:44,440
I mean, if images are upside down on the retina,

1100
01:04:44,440 --> 01:04:47,200
they're upside down on the, on your brain,

1101
01:04:47,200 --> 01:04:51,280
and yet the world is right side up.

1102
01:04:51,280 --> 01:04:52,240
What's going on?

1103
01:04:52,240 --> 01:04:59,840
So I would say about eight or 10 years ago,

1104
01:04:59,840 --> 01:05:09,480
I was asked to review a paper for a high-visibility journal.

1105
01:05:09,480 --> 01:05:12,120
I can't name the journal.

1106
01:05:12,120 --> 01:05:19,400
In which the investigator very, very vociferously

1107
01:05:19,400 --> 01:05:23,040
proclaimed that we have all been wrong

1108
01:05:23,040 --> 01:05:26,680
and that the images are right set up on the retina after all.

1109
01:05:26,680 --> 01:05:29,080
So what was his proof?

1110
01:05:29,080 --> 01:05:35,720
Well, his proof was that he took an ox eye, a slotted ox's eye,

1111
01:05:35,720 --> 01:05:38,440
and cut out a little piece in the back of the eye

1112
01:05:38,440 --> 01:05:41,840
and put a translucent piece of paper over it,

1113
01:05:41,840 --> 01:05:44,880
and then presented a stimulus out there.

1114
01:05:44,880 --> 01:05:47,560
And when you looked at the back of the eye,

1115
01:05:48,040 --> 01:05:50,880
the translucent part, the image was right side up.

1116
01:05:50,880 --> 01:05:53,680
A little bit blurred, but right side up.

1117
01:05:53,680 --> 01:05:57,920
And so he felt that he proved that images are right side up

1118
01:05:57,920 --> 01:06:00,080
on the retina after all.

1119
01:06:00,080 --> 01:06:03,680
Well, I was flabbergasted, and it took me quite a while

1120
01:06:03,680 --> 01:06:07,400
to figure out why this guy got what he got,

1121
01:06:07,400 --> 01:06:09,680
because he'd obviously got what he got.

1122
01:06:09,680 --> 01:06:12,760
So can you guys think of any reason for this?

1123
01:06:12,760 --> 01:06:14,040
How come he got what he got?

1124
01:06:14,480 --> 01:06:16,800
Wait, what did he do?

1125
01:06:16,800 --> 01:06:22,840
He took the eye, and the back he cut out a little opening,

1126
01:06:22,840 --> 01:06:25,200
and put a transparency over it.

1127
01:06:25,200 --> 01:06:29,760
So then anything came in there, you could see what

1128
01:06:29,760 --> 01:06:33,640
would have been normally on the retina surface.

1129
01:06:33,640 --> 01:06:34,920
I mean, it was a clever experiment.

1130
01:06:34,920 --> 01:06:37,040
No question about that.

1131
01:06:37,040 --> 01:06:39,960
So what was wrong with the experiment?

1132
01:06:39,960 --> 01:06:41,680
Well, I'll tell you.

1133
01:06:41,680 --> 01:06:43,520
What was wrong with the experiment

1134
01:06:43,520 --> 01:06:46,880
goes back to what I told you before about the way

1135
01:06:46,880 --> 01:06:50,480
the lens works.

1136
01:06:50,480 --> 01:06:53,920
The lens works so that when you look at something close,

1137
01:06:53,920 --> 01:06:58,560
the muscles of the lens are such that it makes the lens fat.

1138
01:06:58,560 --> 01:07:07,440
And when the muscles relax, it makes the lens thick.

1139
01:07:07,440 --> 01:07:09,200
Sorry, thin.

1140
01:07:09,200 --> 01:07:14,840
So that means, just to be graphic about it,

1141
01:07:14,840 --> 01:07:19,240
is that if you have a thick lens,

1142
01:07:19,240 --> 01:07:23,920
you have a short focal length.

1143
01:07:23,920 --> 01:07:28,800
And if you have a thin lens, you have a much longer focal

1144
01:07:28,800 --> 01:07:31,440
length like that.

1145
01:07:31,440 --> 01:07:40,520
So now, if you look at the back of the eye here,

1146
01:07:40,520 --> 01:07:43,680
where the photoreceptors are, in this case,

1147
01:07:43,680 --> 01:07:45,480
it looks like it's with the upside down.

1148
01:07:45,480 --> 01:07:47,640
But if they were in the same distance here,

1149
01:07:47,640 --> 01:07:50,360
it would be blurred, and it would remain right side up,

1150
01:07:50,360 --> 01:07:53,600
just like it is with a magnifying glass.

1151
01:07:53,600 --> 01:07:59,520
So what this guy didn't know is the rule of how the muscle works

1152
01:07:59,520 --> 01:08:03,480
in the eye to control the lens.

1153
01:08:03,480 --> 01:08:08,800
And when the animal was killed, it no longer

1154
01:08:08,800 --> 01:08:14,240
had the lens at the proper focal depth.

1155
01:08:14,240 --> 01:08:16,840
And therefore, the image was right side up.

1156
01:08:16,840 --> 01:08:20,040
So therefore, guess what happened?

1157
01:08:20,040 --> 01:08:24,240
I wrote back to this high visibility journal saying,

1158
01:08:24,240 --> 01:08:29,360
that this guy is wrong for this reason.

1159
01:08:29,360 --> 01:08:33,000
So that was certainly an interesting experience,

1160
01:08:33,000 --> 01:08:38,840
and that's why I managed to learn a little bit about how

1161
01:08:38,840 --> 01:08:44,640
the lens works in the eye, which is a remarkable way it works,

1162
01:08:44,640 --> 01:08:46,520
since it's so totally different from what

1163
01:08:46,520 --> 01:08:49,840
we are familiar with when it comes to cameras.

1164
01:08:49,840 --> 01:08:55,800
OK, so now let's go to the next step, one down

1165
01:08:55,800 --> 01:09:00,960
from your photoreceptors.

1166
01:09:00,960 --> 01:09:03,880
And we come to the so-called bipolar cells.

1167
01:09:03,880 --> 01:09:07,360
Here's a series of pictures of the so-called cat bipolar

1168
01:09:07,360 --> 01:09:08,360
cells.

1169
01:09:08,360 --> 01:09:11,680
And what was discovered is that the bipolar cells,

1170
01:09:11,680 --> 01:09:13,800
here in the outer plexiform layer,

1171
01:09:13,800 --> 01:09:16,560
they connect with the photoreceptors.

1172
01:09:16,560 --> 01:09:20,000
And the inner plexiform layer, their external projections,

1173
01:09:20,000 --> 01:09:28,720
are such that in the upper layer here, which is IPL layer A,

1174
01:09:28,720 --> 01:09:33,080
the cells there are off cells.

1175
01:09:33,080 --> 01:09:35,960
And the ones that project below this here

1176
01:09:35,960 --> 01:09:37,760
are the so-called on cells.

1177
01:09:37,760 --> 01:09:42,120
So there's a separation of the so-called on and off cells

1178
01:09:42,120 --> 01:09:46,000
at the bipolar cell level in the manner in which they connect.

1179
01:09:46,000 --> 01:09:50,120
And so therefore, those ganglion cells that connect with these

1180
01:09:50,120 --> 01:09:51,520
are off ganglion cells.

1181
01:09:51,520 --> 01:09:57,120
And those that connect with these are on ganglion cells.

1182
01:09:57,120 --> 01:10:00,760
All right, so that's basically out of the bipolar cells.

1183
01:10:00,760 --> 01:10:03,040
And then when you come to the horizontal cells,

1184
01:10:03,040 --> 01:10:05,760
here's an example, a beautiful example,

1185
01:10:05,760 --> 01:10:12,680
of a proscienielo-labeled horizontal cell.

1186
01:10:12,680 --> 01:10:15,840
And if that is then analyzed in detail,

1187
01:10:15,840 --> 01:10:17,600
it looks something like this.

1188
01:10:17,600 --> 01:10:24,480
This particular cell has 15 connections

1189
01:10:24,480 --> 01:10:27,720
with the photoreceptors.

1190
01:10:27,720 --> 01:10:30,280
And these cells, I might as well anticipate.

1191
01:10:30,280 --> 01:10:32,520
We'll talk about that in more detail later.

1192
01:10:32,520 --> 01:10:36,440
They play a central role in providing the surrounding

1193
01:10:36,440 --> 01:10:38,760
inhibition that I described to you

1194
01:10:38,760 --> 01:10:41,080
for those retinal ganglion cells.

1195
01:10:41,080 --> 01:10:43,880
Then if you proceed to the amocrine cells,

1196
01:10:43,880 --> 01:10:45,960
and again, things are so complicated,

1197
01:10:45,960 --> 01:10:51,000
the numerous, numerous amocrine cells, different types.

1198
01:10:51,000 --> 01:10:52,680
And they have been studied extensively.

1199
01:10:52,680 --> 01:10:55,480
They're more than 20.

1200
01:10:55,480 --> 01:10:59,160
And here's a clever experiment I thought I'd point out to you.

1201
01:10:59,160 --> 01:11:03,560
What you can do here, if you look at the so-called cholinergic

1202
01:11:03,560 --> 01:11:06,720
amocrine cells, that's one class,

1203
01:11:06,720 --> 01:11:10,000
you can selectively label them because they're cholinergic.

1204
01:11:10,000 --> 01:11:13,520
And here's an example of what all those lighter blue ones

1205
01:11:14,120 --> 01:11:16,440
are your cholinergic cells.

1206
01:11:16,440 --> 01:11:19,680
And then what you can do, you can put this under a microscope.

1207
01:11:19,680 --> 01:11:22,160
And then you can lower a microelectrode

1208
01:11:22,160 --> 01:11:25,160
into one of these cells, just like I've done here.

1209
01:11:25,160 --> 01:11:26,680
And if you look at this closely, this

1210
01:11:26,680 --> 01:11:28,360
is not the best of pictures.

1211
01:11:28,360 --> 01:11:32,160
This is the labeled, again, close to a yellow labeled cell,

1212
01:11:32,160 --> 01:11:33,160
amocrine cell.

1213
01:11:33,160 --> 01:11:35,320
You can see it has all these processes.

1214
01:11:35,320 --> 01:11:37,920
So then if you look at this on the microscope

1215
01:11:37,920 --> 01:11:42,840
on the high quality, you can draw these out.

1216
01:11:42,840 --> 01:11:45,520
And this is what they look like.

1217
01:11:45,520 --> 01:11:48,400
So this is a cholinergic cell.

1218
01:11:48,400 --> 01:11:49,680
This is another type.

1219
01:11:49,680 --> 01:11:54,080
It's just called A2, which has a lot to do with rods.

1220
01:11:54,080 --> 01:11:56,600
And then we have another class called open immunergic.

1221
01:11:56,600 --> 01:11:58,720
I'm not going to go into detail about it.

1222
01:11:58,720 --> 01:12:01,000
But you can see that each of these now,

1223
01:12:01,000 --> 01:12:04,600
scientific methods enabled you to specify

1224
01:12:04,600 --> 01:12:06,560
what these amocrine cells are like.

1225
01:12:06,560 --> 01:12:08,520
And the basis of that, people have come up

1226
01:12:08,520 --> 01:12:12,240
with ideas what the amocrine system does.

1227
01:12:13,080 --> 01:12:18,200
Now we come to the crux of things in the last few minutes.

1228
01:12:18,200 --> 01:12:21,120
We're going to talk about the electrical responses

1229
01:12:21,120 --> 01:12:22,800
in the retina.

1230
01:12:22,800 --> 01:12:27,120
Now this is going to be, again, fairly complicated.

1231
01:12:27,120 --> 01:12:32,520
This work, again, remarkable work, which in my view

1232
01:12:32,520 --> 01:12:37,560
merits a Nobel Prize, but it has not yet been awarded,

1233
01:12:37,560 --> 01:12:40,560
to John Dowling at Harvard.

1234
01:12:44,160 --> 01:12:47,480
And let me just introduce this by telling you

1235
01:12:47,480 --> 01:12:51,120
how they went about this experiment.

1236
01:12:51,120 --> 01:12:55,280
They realize that the cells in the retina are very, very small.

1237
01:12:55,280 --> 01:12:59,560
And so it's very difficult to record from them intercellulately.

1238
01:12:59,560 --> 01:13:03,320
You have to do that if you want to be able to label them

1239
01:13:03,320 --> 01:13:04,640
anatomically.

1240
01:13:04,640 --> 01:13:09,120
And so they looked around for a species of animal

1241
01:13:09,120 --> 01:13:12,720
that had large cells in the retina.

1242
01:13:12,720 --> 01:13:14,760
And they discovered after a lot of search

1243
01:13:14,760 --> 01:13:20,200
that the so-called mud puppy and the necturus maculosis

1244
01:13:20,200 --> 01:13:25,840
is an animal that has unusually large retinal ganglion cells.

1245
01:13:25,840 --> 01:13:29,280
Then they developed a technique of removing the eye

1246
01:13:29,280 --> 01:13:33,280
from this animal and putting it into a dish,

1247
01:13:33,280 --> 01:13:35,680
because that makes it real stable.

1248
01:13:35,680 --> 01:13:39,400
And then they were able to put an electrode in there,

1249
01:13:39,400 --> 01:13:42,560
define what the cells did functionally

1250
01:13:42,560 --> 01:13:46,480
by shining light on and looking at their activity.

1251
01:13:46,480 --> 01:13:50,200
And then they were able to label the cell

1252
01:13:50,200 --> 01:13:52,400
by injecting a labeling substance.

1253
01:13:52,400 --> 01:13:54,960
So that's what they did.

1254
01:13:54,960 --> 01:13:58,040
So here is the description of that arrangement.

1255
01:13:58,040 --> 01:14:01,600
Here you have an inverted mud puppy retina.

1256
01:14:01,600 --> 01:14:04,280
And here you have a DC recording electrode.

1257
01:14:04,280 --> 01:14:06,760
And what you do then, you look at it in a oscilloscope.

1258
01:14:06,760 --> 01:14:13,760
And at this point, the cell is entered by the DC recording.

1259
01:14:13,760 --> 01:14:16,840
And then once it's inside, cells inside are negatively

1260
01:14:16,840 --> 01:14:21,120
charged with respect to the surround, usually

1261
01:14:21,120 --> 01:14:23,880
up to about 70 millivolts.

1262
01:14:23,880 --> 01:14:26,400
In this case, I have 50 millivolts labeled.

1263
01:14:26,400 --> 01:14:27,920
And so once you're inside the cell,

1264
01:14:27,920 --> 01:14:30,880
you see a sudden drop to a minus level.

1265
01:14:30,880 --> 01:14:34,280
And then if the cell discharges an action potential,

1266
01:14:34,280 --> 01:14:38,600
then you see that, of course, in the oscilloscope.

1267
01:14:38,600 --> 01:14:41,400
And then you can study this cell in all kinds of detail

1268
01:14:41,400 --> 01:14:44,640
as to how it responds, what it responds, and so on,

1269
01:14:44,640 --> 01:14:47,520
to determine what its characteristics are.

1270
01:14:47,520 --> 01:14:50,000
So then, when they did that, they

1271
01:14:50,000 --> 01:14:52,120
made the following central discoveries.

1272
01:14:52,120 --> 01:14:55,400
They discovered, first of all, that receptors

1273
01:14:55,400 --> 01:14:58,360
all hyperpolarized the light.

1274
01:14:58,360 --> 01:15:03,560
Now, this is the opposite of what you would have thought,

1275
01:15:03,560 --> 01:15:07,200
because the principle of the way cells operate,

1276
01:15:07,200 --> 01:15:09,240
and I'm sure you know all this, but I will also

1277
01:15:09,240 --> 01:15:13,040
describe it in a bit more detail in a minute,

1278
01:15:13,040 --> 01:15:18,440
cells, when they hyperpolarize, they

1279
01:15:18,440 --> 01:15:24,240
are less likely to cause a release in neurotransmitters.

1280
01:15:24,240 --> 01:15:28,480
If they depolarize, they will increase

1281
01:15:28,480 --> 01:15:34,440
the likelihood of neurotransmitters being released.

1282
01:15:34,440 --> 01:15:38,280
And by the way, the neurotransmitter for these receptors

1283
01:15:38,280 --> 01:15:39,720
is glutamate.

1284
01:15:39,720 --> 01:15:45,920
And also, which I should emphasize here,

1285
01:15:45,920 --> 01:15:49,480
is that the receptors never give action potentials.

1286
01:15:49,480 --> 01:15:52,200
They only give graded potentials.

1287
01:15:52,200 --> 01:15:54,240
And that's also true for the horizontal cells

1288
01:15:54,240 --> 01:15:56,160
and the bipolar cells.

1289
01:15:56,160 --> 01:15:59,480
Now, they found that all receptors

1290
01:15:59,480 --> 01:16:02,160
hyperpolarized the light, meaning

1291
01:16:02,160 --> 01:16:05,880
that they activate subsequent elements in the retina

1292
01:16:05,880 --> 01:16:09,320
when it gets darker out there, rather than when it gets lighter.

1293
01:16:09,320 --> 01:16:11,840
Which is the opposite that we all would have expected.

1294
01:16:14,600 --> 01:16:16,520
Same thing for the horizontal cells.

1295
01:16:16,520 --> 01:16:18,960
But then, when you come to bipolar cells,

1296
01:16:18,960 --> 01:16:22,200
suddenly you find that there are two different classes.

1297
01:16:22,200 --> 01:16:26,480
One class which mimics what the receptors do.

1298
01:16:26,480 --> 01:16:29,120
The other class that does the opposite.

1299
01:16:29,120 --> 01:16:31,800
And so the bipolar cells of these two types,

1300
01:16:31,800 --> 01:16:36,960
this type has called sine conserving synapses.

1301
01:16:36,960 --> 01:16:40,040
And this type has sine inverting synapses.

1302
01:16:43,600 --> 01:16:47,400
Now, anachronous cells come in various types.

1303
01:16:47,400 --> 01:16:49,160
And some give action potentials.

1304
01:16:49,160 --> 01:16:51,760
And ganglion cells all give action potentials.

1305
01:16:51,760 --> 01:16:54,640
So to go through this again, all receptors

1306
01:16:54,640 --> 01:16:57,280
hyperpolarized the light, horizontal cells,

1307
01:16:57,280 --> 01:17:03,960
or hyperpolarized the light, bipolar cells hyperpolarized

1308
01:17:03,960 --> 01:17:05,960
and have depolarized.

1309
01:17:05,960 --> 01:17:07,720
Now, this is accomplished by the way.

1310
01:17:07,720 --> 01:17:10,000
I'll go into that in more detail later.

1311
01:17:10,000 --> 01:17:13,920
By virtue of having different kinds

1312
01:17:13,920 --> 01:17:19,800
of neurotransmitter receptor sites.

1313
01:17:19,800 --> 01:17:21,560
And I'll talk about that in a lot of detail

1314
01:17:21,560 --> 01:17:24,120
when you talk about the unenolved cells.

1315
01:17:24,120 --> 01:17:25,760
OK, and then we have the anachronous cells,

1316
01:17:25,760 --> 01:17:27,240
some hyperpolarized, some depolarized,

1317
01:17:27,240 --> 01:17:28,920
and some give action potentials.

1318
01:17:28,920 --> 01:17:32,560
And all ganglion cells give action potentials.

1319
01:17:32,560 --> 01:17:35,440
So that is a major, major discovery.

1320
01:17:35,440 --> 01:17:38,360
And not only major, but totally unexpected

1321
01:17:38,360 --> 01:17:41,000
from what one would have thought.

1322
01:17:41,440 --> 01:17:46,520
So now, let me just explain this in a bit more detail.

1323
01:17:46,520 --> 01:17:52,800
If you go inside a cell, then if the cell is activated,

1324
01:17:52,800 --> 01:17:57,240
it can be either get what you see

1325
01:17:57,240 --> 01:17:59,800
as an excitatory postsynaptic potential, which

1326
01:17:59,800 --> 01:18:01,960
goes from minus to what's plus.

1327
01:18:01,960 --> 01:18:05,880
Or you can get an IPSP, an inhibitory postsynaptic

1328
01:18:05,880 --> 01:18:07,120
potential.

1329
01:18:07,120 --> 01:18:10,240
And this discovery was made by Echols,

1330
01:18:10,240 --> 01:18:12,760
which was a remarkably discovery in which he received

1331
01:18:12,760 --> 01:18:17,240
the Nobel Prize some years back.

1332
01:18:17,240 --> 01:18:21,200
All right, so now, if we look at those cells which

1333
01:18:21,200 --> 01:18:23,160
give action potentials, what happens

1334
01:18:23,160 --> 01:18:24,960
is when you get an EPSP, that's why

1335
01:18:24,960 --> 01:18:28,200
it's called excitatory postsynaptic potential,

1336
01:18:28,200 --> 01:18:31,360
then you increase the likelihood that an action

1337
01:18:31,360 --> 01:18:33,280
potential is generated.

1338
01:18:33,280 --> 01:18:37,040
That will send its signal down the axon

1339
01:18:37,040 --> 01:18:42,000
to the next location of the cells.

1340
01:18:42,000 --> 01:18:44,960
All right, so that's the basic process.

1341
01:18:44,960 --> 01:18:47,840
So again, to reiterate, photoreceptors hyperpolarize

1342
01:18:47,840 --> 01:18:49,080
the light.

1343
01:18:49,080 --> 01:18:51,240
Therefore, glutamate is released when

1344
01:18:51,240 --> 01:18:54,320
there is a decrease in illumination,

1345
01:18:54,320 --> 01:18:58,280
the opposite of what anyone would have expected.

1346
01:18:58,280 --> 01:19:02,720
All right, so that, I think, brings us

1347
01:19:02,720 --> 01:19:04,320
to what I wanted to cover today.

1348
01:19:04,320 --> 01:19:06,360
I'll wait until next time to talk

1349
01:19:06,360 --> 01:19:09,440
about the lateral genetic net nucleus, which is very brief.

1350
01:19:09,440 --> 01:19:12,640
But let me go through the photoreceptor basics again.

1351
01:19:12,640 --> 01:19:16,480
All photoreceptors hyperpolarize the light.

1352
01:19:16,480 --> 01:19:20,760
Depolarization of the photoreceptor releases glutamate.

1353
01:19:20,760 --> 01:19:24,480
Photon absorption by the photopigment results

1354
01:19:24,480 --> 01:19:28,320
in isomerization of the chromophore from 11, 6 to 11

1355
01:19:28,320 --> 01:19:29,000
trends.

1356
01:19:29,000 --> 01:19:32,520
This is what I told you to think about in a very simple

1357
01:19:32,520 --> 01:19:40,560
fashion to say that the photoreceptor molecules,

1358
01:19:40,560 --> 01:19:46,000
rhodopsin in this case, either is bleached or unbleached.

1359
01:19:46,000 --> 01:19:49,080
So once it's bleached, further light has no effect on it.

1360
01:19:49,080 --> 01:19:53,080
And at any point, given whatever the level of illumination is,

1361
01:19:53,080 --> 01:19:56,600
a certain percentage of these rhodopsin molecules

1362
01:19:56,600 --> 01:19:59,280
are bleached, and a certain percentage is unbleached.

1363
01:19:59,280 --> 01:20:03,160
The darker it is, the more are unbleached.

1364
01:20:03,160 --> 01:20:06,120
All right, then we have these two classes

1365
01:20:06,120 --> 01:20:08,200
that we talked about, the on and the off cells

1366
01:20:08,200 --> 01:20:11,120
that we will discuss in some detail.

1367
01:20:11,120 --> 01:20:14,080
The synaptic junction of bipolar is assigned conserving,

1368
01:20:14,080 --> 01:20:15,320
as I had just said.

1369
01:20:15,320 --> 01:20:18,840
And that of the unbipolar is assigned inverting.

1370
01:20:18,840 --> 01:20:22,840
Now, the unbipolar receptor is, I should mention that now,

1371
01:20:22,840 --> 01:20:29,080
and I will deal with it in detail, is called the M-glue R6.

1372
01:20:29,080 --> 01:20:31,960
Its activation leads to closing of channels

1373
01:20:31,960 --> 01:20:34,080
causing hyperpolarization.

1374
01:20:34,080 --> 01:20:38,000
So that's the basic layout, then, of the photoreceptors.

1375
01:20:38,000 --> 01:20:42,160
And I will now stop at this point.

1376
01:20:42,160 --> 01:20:44,000
And next time, you'll talk a little bit

1377
01:20:44,000 --> 01:20:47,760
about the nitrogenic nucleus.

1378
01:20:47,760 --> 01:20:50,640
And then we're going to move on to talk about the cortex.

1379
01:20:50,640 --> 01:20:54,320
And then if you look at your assignment sheet,

1380
01:20:54,320 --> 01:20:58,080
then you can see here that on September 16th,

1381
01:20:58,080 --> 01:21:00,480
we're going to talk about the on and off channels.

1382
01:21:00,480 --> 01:21:02,560
And then we'll deal in more detail

1383
01:21:02,560 --> 01:21:04,840
with these rather complex things, which

1384
01:21:04,840 --> 01:21:08,040
are pretty hard to remember about hyperpolarization,

1385
01:21:08,040 --> 01:21:12,560
depolarization, glutamate, glutamate, receptor sites,

1386
01:21:12,560 --> 01:21:13,360
and so on.

1387
01:21:13,360 --> 01:21:15,120
OK?

1388
01:21:15,120 --> 01:21:16,760
Thank you.

